nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nFor each \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place a horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\le i\\le i,\\;i<j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle covering the squares to the right of the hole \\((i,i)\\).  \n\nFor each \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place a vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nC_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\le j\\le j,\\;j-1<i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is an \\((n-j+1)\\times1\\) rectangle covering the squares below the hole \\((j-1,j)\\).  \n\nThe rectangles \\(\\{R_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint, the rectangles \\(\\{C_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, and no \\(R_i\\) meets any \\(C_j\\) (they lie on opposite sides of the diagonal).  \nTogether they cover every square of the board except the \\(n\\) diagonal holes.  \nHence we have a tiling with  \n\n\\[\n| \\{R_i\\}|+| \\{C_j\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFix an arbitrary permutation \\(p\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=p(i)-1\\qquad\\text{(number of squares left of the hole in row }i\\text{)},\n\\]\n\\[\nr_i:=n-p(i)\\qquad\\text{(number of squares right of the hole in row }i\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nBoth \\(\\ell_i\\) and \\(r_i\\) belong to \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nBecause \\(p\\) is a bijection, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is exactly \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); the same holds for \\(\\{r_i\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 3.1  Squares that force at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles  \n\nConsider the set  \n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,\\ell_i)\\mid \\ell_i>0\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(i\\) with \\(\\ell_i>0\\) the cell \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) is the **rightmost** square of the left part of row \\(i\\).  \nSince the values \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct, the cells of \\(L\\) lie in distinct columns.\n\n**Lemma 1.** A rectangle that avoids all holes can contain at most one cell of \\(L\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(Q\\) cont