   \\[\n     S_k=\\{k+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,k\\},\\qquad k=1,2,\\dots ,n-1,\n     \\]\n     each of size \\((n-k)\\times k\\).  \n   - The \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint, axis\u2011aligned, and together cover every unit square except the diagonal ones.  \n\n4. **Conclusion:**  \n   - The construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, matching the lower bound.  \n   - For \\(n=2025\\) we obtain \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\) tiles, which is therefore minimal.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(\\sigma(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Left side lower bound).  \nFor every row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) the square \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) must belong to a distinct tile. Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover all squares lying **left** of the uncovered squares.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFix a row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\). The square \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) is the rightmost covered square in that row.  \nConsider the tile \\(T\\) that contains \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\). Because a tile cannot contain the uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), the right side of \\(T\\) cannot lie to the right of column \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\); hence the right side of \\(T\\) is exactly the vertical grid line \\(x=\\sigma(i)\\).  \n\nLet \\(I\\) be the set of rows covered by \\(T\\). For any row \\(r\\in I\\) we must have \\(\\sigma(r) > \\sigma(i)-1\\); otherwise column \\(\\sigma(r)\\) would lie at or left of the right side of \\(T\\) and the uncovered square \\((r,\\sigma(r))\\) would be inside \\(T\\), a contradiction. Hence\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=\\min_{r\\in I}\\sigma(r).\n\\]\nThus row \\(i\\) is the unique row of \\(I\\) attaining this minimum.  \n\nIf two different rows \\(i\\neq i'\\) with \\(\\sigma(i),\\sigma(i')>1\\) were covered by the same tile, that tile would have a single minimal \\(\\sigma\\)-value, contradicting the uniqueness just proved. Hence the tiles covering the squares \\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) for distinct rows \\(i\\) are distinct.  \n\nSince exactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) (the column\u2011\\(1\\) hole), there are \\(n-1\\) rows with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\). Consequently at least \\(n-1\\) distinct tiles are needed to cover all squares left of the uncovered squares. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Right side lower bound).  \nFor every row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) the square \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\) must belong to a distinct tile. Hence at least \\(n-1\\) tiles are required to cover all squares lying **right** of the uncovered squares.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument is symmetric to Lemma\u202f1. The leftmost covered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\). Any tile containing it cannot extend left of column \\(\\sigma(i)+1\\); therefore its left side coincides with the grid line \\(x=\\sigma(i)+1\\). By the same minimal\u2011\\(\\sigma\\) reasoning, the tile is uniquely associated with row \\(i\\). Distinct rows with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) give distinct tiles. Exactly one row satisfies \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); thus there are \\(n-1\\) such rows, yielding at least \\(n-1\\) tiles. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary (Global lower bound).  \nAny tiling satisfying the problem\u2019s condition uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound.\n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. set \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered set is \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).\n\nDefine for each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dot