"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Permutation model.**  \n   Let \\(n=2025\\).  In each row \\(i\\) there is exactly one uncovered unit square; denote its column by \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  Because each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, \\(\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is a permutation.\n\n2. **Lower bound (two staircase corners).**  \n   \u2013 For the region **above** the diagonal (the squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i<j\\)) consider the \\(n-1\\) squares \\((i,i+1)\\) (the left\u2011most squares of that region).  \n   \u2013 Show that a single axis\u2011aligned rectangle cannot contain two distinct squares of the form \\((i,i+1)\\); otherwise the rectangle would also contain a diagonal hole, which is forbidden.  \n   \u2013 Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover the upper region.  \n\n   \u2013 By symmetry, the \\(n-1\\) squares \\((i,i-1)\\) (the right\u2011most squares of the region **below** the diagonal) also require at least \\(n-1\\) distinct rectangles.  \n\n   \u2013 Consequently any admissible tiling uses at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n3. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Place the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \n\n   \u2013 **Upper region:** for each column \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) take the vertical strip  \n\n     \\[\n     T_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n     \\]\n\n     a rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\).  \n\n   \u2013 **Lower region:** for each row \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) take the horizontal strip  \n\n     \\[\n     S_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n     \\]\n\n     a rectangle of size \\(1\\times(i-1)\\).  \n\n   The \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_j\\}\\cup\\{S_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint, lie entirely on one side of the diagonal, and together cover every unit square except the diagonal ones.  \n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2=4048\\).  \n\n5. **Explicit example for the smallest non\u2011zero case.**  \n   For a \\(2\\times2\\) grid (\\(n=2\\)) the uncovered squares are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  The two remaining squares \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) must each be covered by a separate \\(1\\times1\\) tile, giving exactly \\(2=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nFor each row \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)\\) denote by \\(\\sigma(i)\\) the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Upper\u2011region corner squares).  \n\nFor every \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the square  \n\n\\[\nc_i^{\\text{up}}=(i,i+1)\n\\]\n\nlies in the region \\(\\{(r,c):r<c\\}\\) (the part of the board strictly above the diagonal).  \nNo axis\u2011aligned rectangle can contain two distinct squares \\(c_i^{\\text{up}}\\) and \\(c_j^{\\text{up}}\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\) contains both \\(c_i^{\\text{up}}=(i,i+1)\\) and \\(c_j^{\\text{up}}=(j,j+1)\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThen the row interval of \\(R\\) contains \\(\\{i,i+1,\\dots ,j\\}\\) and the column interval contains \\(\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,j+1\\}\\).  \nConsequently the square \\((j,i+1)\\) belongs to \\(R\\) (it lies in row \\(j\\) and column \\(i+1\\)).  \nBut column \\(i+1\\) contains only the rows \\(1,\\dots ,i\\) in the upper region; the square \\((j,i+1)\\) is a diagonal hole \\((j,i+1)\\) with \\(j>i\\), i.e. it is uncovered.  \nThus \\(R\\) would contain an uncovered square, contradicting the definition of a tile. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Lower\u2011region corner squares).  \n\nFor every \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the square  \n\n\\[\nc_i^{\\text{low}}=(i,i-1)\n\\]\n\nlies in the region \\(\\{(r,c):r>c\\}\\) (the part strictly below the diagonal).  \nNo axis\u2011aligned rectangle can contain two distinct squares \\(c_i^{\\text{low}}\\) and \\(c_j^{\\text{low}}\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument is symmetric to Lemma\u202f1: if a rectangle contained \\((i,i-1)\\) and \\((j,j-1)\\) with \\(i<j\\), then it would also contain \\((i,j-1)\\), which is a diagonal hole, a contradiction. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Corollary (Global lower bound).  \n\nThe set \\(\\{c_i^{\\text{up}}:1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\) consists of \\(n-1\\) distinct squares, each of which must belong to a different tile by Lemma\u202f1.  \nSimilarly, the set \\(\\{c_i^{\\text{low}}:2\\le i\\le n\\}\\) consists of another \\(n-1\\) distinct squares, each belonging to a different tile by Lemma\u202f2.  \n\nHence any tiling satisfying the problem\u2019s condition uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles. \u220e\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound.\n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=i\\qquad\\text{for all }i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nDefine the following rectangles.\n\n*Upper region.*  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nT_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(T_j\\) is a rectangle of size \\((j-1)\\times1\\); it consists of the vertical strip of column \\(j\\) that lies above the diagonal.\n\n*Lower region.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S_i\\) is a rectangle of size \\(1\\times(i-1)\\); it consists of the horizontal strip of row \\(i\\) that lies below the diagonal.\n\n*Disjointness.*  \nIf \\(j\\neq j'\\) then \\(T_j\\) and \\(T_{j'}\\) lie in different columns, so they are disjoint.  \nIf \\(i\\neq i'\\) then \\(S_i\\) and \\(S_{i'}\\) lie in different rows, so they are disjoint.  \nA square belonging to both some \\(T_j\\) and some \\(S_i\\) would have to satisfy simultaneously  \n\n\\[\ni\\le j-1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad j\\le i-1,\n\\]\n\nwhich is impossible.  Hence the families \\(\\{T_j\\}\\) and \\(\\{S_i\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery square \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r<c\\) (above the diagonal) belongs to the unique rectangle \\(T_c\\), because \\(c\\ge2\\) and \\(1\\le r\\le c-1\\).  \nEvery square \\((r,c)\\) with \\(r>c\\) (below the diagonal) belongs to the unique rectangle \\(S_r\\), because \\(r\\ge2\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le r-1\\).  \nThe diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\) are precisely the uncovered squares.  Thus the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\cup\\{S_i\\}_{i=2}^{n}\\) form a valid tiling.\n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{T_j\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{S_i\\}\\,|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof.\n\nThe lower\u2011bound corollary shows that no admissible tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nThe explicit construction above achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Explicit example for the smallest non\u2011zero case.\n\nFor a \\(2\\times2\\) grid (\\(n=2\\)) the uncovered squares must be \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \nThe remaining squares \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) cannot be covered by a single rectangle, so each is covered by a separate \\(1\\times1\\) tile.  \nThus the tiling uses exactly \\(2=2n-2\\) tiles, illustrating the construction in the smallest non\u2011trivial instance. \u220e"
