g the “left‑most’’ and “right‑most’’ intervals that appear when we scan the rows from top to bottom we obtain at least \\(2n-2\\) distinct maximal rectangles that are forced to appear in any partition.\n\n3. **Construction (Lemma 2).**  \n   For the “staircase’’ permutation \\(\\pi(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal) one can tile the complement with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles:  \n   * For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a rectangle covering the cells \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\; k+1\\le j\\le n\\}\\) (a “upper‑right’’ block).  \n   * For each \\(k=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a rectangle covering the cells \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid k\\le i\\le n,\\; 1\\le j\\le k-1\\}\\) (a “lower‑left’’ block).  \n   These \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are pairwise disjoint and together cover every cell except the diagonal holes.\n\n4. **Verification.**  \n   A point‑by‑point check shows that each cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\) belongs to exactly one of the two families of rectangles above, while each diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) is uncovered. Hence the construction satisfies the required condition.\n\n5. **Implications for \\(n=2025\\).**  \n   Lemma 1 gives the universal lower bound \\(\\displaystyle \\text{tiles}\\ge 2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).  \n   Lemma 2 shows that for the diagonal permutation the bound is attainable for all \\(n\\) (the construction works for any size). Consequently the minimum number of tiles for the \\(2025\\times2025\\) grid is **exactly** \\(4048\\).\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 2\\) be an integer (later we will set \\(n=2025\\)).  \nA placement of rectangular tiles satisfying the problem’s conditions is equivalent to a partition of the set  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j\\neq\\pi(i)\\}\n\\]\n\ninto axis‑parallel rectangles, where \\(\\pi\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) describing the uncovered squares.  \nWe prove that the smallest possible number of rectangles in such a partition is \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (Lower bound)\n\n*Statement.*  \nFor any permutation \\(\\pi\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) the set \\(C\\) cannot be partitioned into fewer than \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor each row \\(i\\) define the two intervals  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{j\\mid 1\\le j<\\pi(i)\\},\\qquad\nR_i=\\{j\\mid \\pi(i)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\pi(i)=1\\) then \\(L_i=\\varnothing\\); if \\(\\pi(i)=n\\) then \\(R_i=\\varnothing\\).  \nEvery cell of \\(C\\) lies either in some \\(L_i\\) or in some \\(R_i\\).\n\nConsider a rectangle \\(R\\) that occupies rows \\(i_1,\\dots ,i_2\\) (with \\(i_1\\le i_2\\)) and columns \\(c_1,\\dots ,c_2\\) (with \\(c_1\\le c_2\\)).  \nBecause \\(R\\) cannot contain any uncovered square, for each row \\(i\\in[i_1,i_2]\\) we must have either  \n\n\\[\nc_2<\\pi(i)\\qquad\\text{(the whole rectangle lies left of the hole in row }i\\text{)},\n\\]\n\nor  \n\n\\[\nc_1>\\pi(i)\\qquad\\text{(the whole rectangle lies right of the hole in row }i\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\nHence the column interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) is either a subset of \\(\\bigcap_{i=i_1}^{i_2}L_i\\) or a subset of \\(\\bigcap_{i=i_1}^{i_2}R_i\\).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL^{\\max }_k=\\bigcap_{i=1}^{k}L_i,\\qquad\nR^{\\max }_k=\\bigcap_{i=k}^{n}R_i\\qquad (1\\le k\\le n).\n\\]\n\nBoth families are nested: \\(L^{\\max }_1\\supseteq L^{\\max }_2\\supseteq\\cdots\\supseteq L^{\\max }_n\\) and\n\\(R^{\\max }_1\\subseteq R^{\\max }_2\\subseteq\\cdots\\subseteq R^{\\max }_n\\).  \nMoreover, for each \\(k\\) the set \\(L^{\\max }_k\\) is non‑empty unless \\(\\pi(k)=1\\); similarly \\(R^{\\max }_k\\) is non‑empty unless \\(\\pi(k)=n\\).\n\nNow take any partition of \\(C\\) into rectangles.  \nEach rectangle that lies entirely to the left of all holes in rows \\(1,\\dots ,k\\) must be contained in \\(L^{\\max }_k\\); conversely, any rectangle that lies entirely to the right of all holes in rows \\(k,\\dots ,n\\) must be contained in \\(R^{\\max }_k\\).\n\nBecause the