t of the uncovered cell, so no uncovered square lies inside \\(R_i^{\\text{right}}\\).  \n- In \\(R_i^{\\text{below}}\\) the column is \\(\\{i\\}\\) and the row set is \\(\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\); the uncovered cell in column \\(i\\) is \\((i,i)\\), which lies above the rectangle, so again no uncovered square is contained.\n\n*Verification that the rectangles cover exactly the complement.*\n\nTake any off‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\).\n\n- If \\(i<j\\) then \\(j\\in\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\((i,j)\\in R_i^{\\text{right}}\\).  \n- If \\(i>j\\) then \\(i\\in\\{j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\((i,j)\\in R_j^{\\text{below}}\\).\n\nThus every cell of \\(C\\) belongs to exactly one of the rectangles, and the rectangles are pairwise disjoint.  \nThere are \\(n-1\\) choices for \\(i\\), and for each we have two rectangles, so the total number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{minimum number of tiles }\\le 2n-2 } .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Lower bound – at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles are necessary\n\nWe prove that any tiling of the complement of a permutation matrix needs at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.  \nThe proof proceeds by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Base case \\(n=2\\)\n\nThe board consists of four unit squares.  \nThe uncovered squares must be opposite corners, say \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\).  \nThe two remaining squares \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) are not adjacent, so each requires its own \\(1\\times1\\) tile.  \nThus any tiling uses exactly two tiles, and \\(2n-2=2\\).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Inductive step\n\nAssume the statement holds for size \\(n-1\\) (with \\(n\\ge3\\)).  \nConsider a tiling of an \\(n\\times n\\) board satisfying the problem’s condition.  \nLet \\(\\sigma\\) be the associated permutation and let the uncovered square in row \\(r\\) be \\((r,\\sigma(r))\\).\n\nBecause row \\(r\\) contains exactly one uncovered cell, the remaining \\(n-1\\) cells of that row split into two non‑empty blocks:\n\n\\[\nL_r=\\{(r,j)\\mid 1\\le j<\\sigma(r)\\},\\qquad\nR_r=\\{(r,j)\\mid \\sigma(r)<j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery tile that meets row \\(r\\) must lie **entirely** inside one of these blocks; otherwise it would contain the uncovered cell \\((r,\\sigma(r))\\).  \nConsequently the set of tiles meeting row \\(r\\) splits into two non‑empty families:\n\n* \\( \\mathcal{L}_r\\): tiles covering cells of \\(L_r\\);\n* \\( \\mathcal{R}_r\\): tiles covering cells of \\(R_r\\).\n\nThus at least one tile belongs to \\(\\mathcal{L}_r\\) and at least one tile belongs to \\(\\mathcal{R}_r\\); in particular\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{L}_r|+|\\mathcal{R}_r|\\ge 2. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nNow delete row \\(r\\) and column \\(\\sigma(r)\\) from the board, and delete all tiles belonging to \\(\\mathcal{L}_r\\cup\\mathcal{R}_r\\).  \nThe remaining tiles still form a tiling of the \\((n-1)\\times (n-1)\\) board obtained after the deletion, and in that smaller board each row and each column also contains exactly one uncovered square (the original uncovered squares except the one in row \\(r\\) and column \\(\\sigma(r)\\)).  \n\nBy the induction hypothesis, any such tiling of size \\(n-1\\) needs at least\n\n\\[\n2\\,(n-1)-2=2n-4\n\\]\n\ntiles.  \nTherefore the original tiling uses at least\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal{L}_r|+|\\mathcal{R}_r| \\;+\\; (2n-4)\n\\]\n\ntiles.  Using (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\text{number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2+(2n-4)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus every admissible tiling of an \\(n\\times n\\) board uses at least \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles and proved that no tiling can use fewer.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025