st remain uncovered,\na contradiction.  \nHence **no rectangle can contain simultaneously a cell above and a cell below the diagonal**.\n\nConsequently each rectangle lies entirely in one of the two disjoint regions  \n\n\\[\nU^{+}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i<j\\le n\\}\\qquad\\text{(upper triangle)},\n\\]\n\\[\nU^{-}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j<i\\le n\\}\\qquad\\text{(lower triangle)}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling of the whole board splits into a tiling of \\(U^{+}\\) and a tiling of \\(U^{-}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3. Lower bound for the upper triangle\n\nDefine the set of **right\u2011corner cells**\n\\[\nC^{+}=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\subseteq U^{+}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nThere are exactly \\(|C^{+}|=n-1\\) such cells.\n\n\\[\n\\textbf{Claim.}\\quad\\text{A rectangle contained in }U^{+}\\text{ can contain at most one cell of }C^{+}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R\\subseteq U^{+}\\) contains two distinct cells\n\\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((k,k+1)\\) with \\(i<k\\).  \nSince \\(R\\) contains these two cells, its row interval contains \\([i,k]\\) and its column\ninterval contains \\([i+1,k+1]\\).  \nBecause \\(i+1\\le k\\), the diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) belongs to both intervals,\nhence \\((i+1,i+1)\\in R\\).  This diagonal cell is uncovered by (2), contradicting the\nassumption that \\(R\\) is part of a valid tiling.  \u220e\n\nSince the rectangles covering \\(U^{+}\\) must cover every cell of \\(C^{+}\\) and each\nsuch rectangle can cover at most one element of \\(C^{+}\\), we need at least \\(|C^{+}|=n-1\\)\nrectangles to tile \\(U^{+}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4. Lower bound for the lower triangle\n\nBy symmetry, define the **lower\u2011corner cells**\n\\[\nC^{-}=\\{(i,i-1)\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}\\subseteq U^{-},\n\\]\nagain \\(|C^{-}|=n-1\\).  \nExactly the same argument as in the claim shows that any rectangle contained in\n\\(U^{-}\\) can contain at most one cell of \\(C^{-}\\).  Hence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles\nare required to tile \\(U^{-}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5. Global lower bound\n\nCombining the two parts,\n\\[\n\\text{any tiling of the whole board uses at least }(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\text{ rectangles.}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6. Construction attaining the bound\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define two rectangles:\n\n* a **horizontal** rectangle\n  \\[\n  H_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n  \\tag{6}\n  \\]\n  i.e. the whole row \\(i\\) to the right of the diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\);\n\n* a **vertical** rectangle\n  \\[\n  V_i=\\{(j,i)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n  i.e. the whole column \\(i\\) below the diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Verification.*  \n\n1. **Disjointness.**  \n   - Each \\(H_i\\) occupies only row \\(i\\); each \\(V_j\\) occupies only column \\(j\\).  \n   - The only possible intersection of \\(H_i\\) and \\(V_j\\) would be the cell \\((i,j)\\).  \n     If \\(i<j\\), then \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\) but \\(V_j\\) uses rows \\(>j\\), so \\((i,j)\\notin V_j\\).  \n     If \\(i>j\\), then \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\) but \\(H_i\\) uses columns \\(>i\\), so \\((i,j)\\notin H_i\\).  \n     If \\(i=j\\), the two rectangles are disjoint by definition.  \n     Hence all rectangles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n2. **Avoidance of diagonal cells.**  \n   - For any \\(i\\), the columns of \\(H_i\\) are \\(\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), all strictly larger than the diagonal column \\(i\\); thus \\((i,i)\\notin H_i\\).  \n   - For any \\(i\\), the rows of \\(V_i\\) are \\(\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), all strictly larger than the diagonal row \\(i\\); thus \\((i,i)\\notin V_i\\).\n\n3. **Coverage of all off\u2011diagonal cells.**  \n   - Let \\((p,q)\\) be a cell with \\(p<q\\) (i.e. in \\(U^{+}\\)). Then \\(q\\ge p+1\\), so \\((p,q)\\in H_p\\).  \n   - Let \\((p,q)\\) be a cell with \\(p>q\\) (i.e. in \\(U^{-}\\)). Then \\(p\\ge q+1\\), so \\((p,q)\\in V_q\\).  \n   Hence every off\u2011diagonal cell belongs to exactly one of the rectangles.\n\nThere are \\(n-1\\) horizontal rectangles and \\(n-1\\) vertical rectangles, for a total of\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\ntiles.  This construction shows that the lower bound (5) is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 7. Conclusion\n\nThe minimal possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).  Substituting \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\cdot2025-2=4048}.\n\\]\n\nThus Matilda can achieve the required condition with \\(4048\\) tiles, and no arrangement can use fewer.\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n* The reduction to the diagonal (Section\u202f1) is justified and does not alter the tile count.\n* The \u201cno rectangle crosses the diagonal\u2019\u2019 argument (Section\u202f2) is fully rigorous.\n* The corner\u2011cell lower\u2011bound arguments (Sections\u202f3\u20135) correctly prove that each rectangle can contain at most one corner cell, and that all corner cells must be covered.\n* The construction (Section\u202f6) is explicitly checked for disjointness, avoidance of diagonal cells, and complete coverage of all off\u2011diagonal cells.\n* All mathematical statements are enclosed in TeX delimiters and the final answer appears in \\boxed{} form."
