east \\(n-1\\) rectangles. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Right‑side lower bound)  \nAn analogous argument applied to the numbers  \n\n\\[\nb_i:=n-\\sigma(i)\\qquad(0\\le b_i\\le n-1)\n\\]\n\n(which also form the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\)) shows that any tiling of \\(R\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lower bound for the whole board  \n\nBy Lemma 1 the tiles covering \\(\\mathcal C\\) split into a family covering \\(L\\) and a family covering \\(R\\).  \nLemmas 2 and 3 give  \n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nThus **any** placement satisfying the problem’s condition uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left side.* For each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\) place a horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{L}}:=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle lying entirely in \\(L\\).\n\n*Right side.* For each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\le n-1\\) place a horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{R}}:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) rectangle lying entirely in \\(R\\).\n\nThe two families are pairwise disjoint, none contains a diagonal cell, and together they cover every non‑diagonal cell.  \nThe number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left tiles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right tiles}}=2n-2.\n\\]\n\nHence the lower bound is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Final computation  \n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimal number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------