ndition needs at least \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**  \n   The upper and lower bounds coincide; therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nThe grid consists of the unit squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix  \n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered square, there is a function  \n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]  \nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \nThe same condition for columns forces \\(\\sigma\\) to be a bijection; hence \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nDenote the set of uncovered squares by  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other squares must be covered by tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Upper bound: a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) consider the two intervals of columns that are not holes:\n\n* left interval \\(L_i=\\{1,\\dots ,\\sigma(i)-1\\}\\) (empty if \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\));\n* right interval \\(R_i=\\{\\sigma(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (empty if \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\)).\n\nPlace a horizontal rectangular tile covering exactly the squares of \\(L_i\\) (if non‑empty) and another tile covering exactly the squares of \\(R_i\\) (if non‑empty).  \nThese tiles lie in distinct rows, so they are pairwise disjoint and together cover every square outside \\(U\\).\n\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, there is precisely one index \\(i_0\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_0)=1\\) (no left tile) and one index \\(i_1\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_1)=n\\) (no right tile).  \nHence the total number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{i:\\sigma(i)>1\\}\\bigr|+\\bigl|\\{i:\\sigma(i)<n\\}\\bigr|\n=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles always exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound: at least