th \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define the left interval  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\sigma(i)-1\\}\n\\qquad(\\text{empty if }\\sigma(i)=1),\n\\]\n\nand the right interval  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\\{\\sigma(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\qquad(\\text{empty if }\\sigma(i)=n).\n\\]\n\nPlace a horizontal rectangular tile covering exactly the squares \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\in L_i\\}\\) (if \\(L_i\\neq\\varnothing\\)) and a horizontal tile covering exactly the squares \\(\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\in R_i\\}\\) (if \\(R_i\\neq\\varnothing\\)).  \n\nThese tiles lie in distinct rows, never overlap, and together cover every square outside \\(U\\).  \n\nSince \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation, there is precisely one index \\(i_0\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_0)=1\\) (no left tile) and precisely one index \\(i_1\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_1)=n\\) (no right tile).  Consequently the total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl|\\{i:\\sigma(i)>1\\}\\bigr|+\\bigl|\\{i:\\sigma(i)<n\\}\\bigr|\n=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles always exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound: at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles are required  \n\n##### 3.1  Reduction to the case \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\)\n\nRename the rows by a permutation \\(\\tau\\) so that  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(\\tau(1))<\\sigma(\\tau(2))<\\dots <\\sigma(\\tau(n)).\n\\]\n\nAfter this relabelling the uncovered squares become  \n\n\\[\nU'=\\{(k,k)\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the diagonal squares.  Relabelling rows does not change the geometry of any tile; a rectangle remains a rectangle and still avoids all uncovered squares.  Hence it suffices to prove the lower bound under the assumption  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)=i\\qquad\\text{for all }i.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom now on we work with the grid in which the uncovered squares are exactly the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n##### 3.2  A set of forced covered squares  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nS_L=\\{(i,i-1)\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad\nS_R=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\},\n\\]\nand let \\(S=S_L\\cup S_R\