{p_i:\\sigma(i)>1\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly one cell in each column \\(c\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); hence\n\\(|P|=n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nA rectangle \\(R=I\\times J\\subseteq L\\) contains at most one element of \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume that \\(R\\) contains two distinct left‑corner cells\n\\((i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) and \\((k,\\sigma(k)-1)\\) with \\(i<k\\).\nSince \\(J\\) is an interval of columns, it contains both\n\\(\\sigma(i)-1\\) and \\(\\sigma(k)-1\\); consequently it also contains the\ncolumn \\(\\sigma(i)\\) (because \\(\\sigma(i)-1<\\sigma(i)\\le\\sigma(k)-1\\) or\n\\(\\sigma(i)=\\sigma(k)-1\\)).  Hence the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) belongs to\n\\(I\\times J\\), contradicting the fact that \\(I\\times J\\subseteq L\\) (the\nhole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) is not in \\(L\\)).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause each rectangle covering \\(L\\) can contain at most one element of \\(P\\),\nat least \\(|P|=n-1\\) rectangles are necessary to cover all of \\(L\\):\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }L\\;\\ge\\;n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A lower bound for the right side \\(R\\)\n\nDefine for each row with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) the **right‑corner cell**\n\n\\[\nq_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\in R_i .\n\\]\n\nExactly one such cell lies in each column \\(c\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\); thus the\nset  \n\n\\[\nQ:=\\{q_i:\\sigma(i)<n\\}\n\\]\n\nhas size \\(n-1\\).\n\nThe same argument as in Lemma 1 shows that any rectangle contained in \\(R\\)\n\\) can contain at most one element of \\(Q\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles covering }R\\;\\ge\\;n-1. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Global lower bound\n\nAdding (2) and (3) and using (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the uncovered squares are the\nmain diagonal).  For each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\) place a horizontal tile\n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,j):1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle covering the whole left part\n\\(L_i\\).  For each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\le n-1\\) place a horizontal tile\n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,j):i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\na \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) rectangle covering the right part \\(R_i\\).\n\nThe tiles \\(T_i^{\\text{left}}\\;(i=2,\\dots ,n)\\) are pairwise disjoint,\nthe tiles \\(T_i^{\\text{right}}\\;(i=1,\\dots ,n-1)\\) are pairwise\ndisjoint, and a left‑tile never meets a right‑tile because their column\nintervals are separated by the diagonal hole \\((i,i)\\).  Hence the\n\\(2n-2\\) tiles cover every unit square except the diagonal ones, and no two\ntiles overlap.\n\nThus the bound (4) is tight, and the minimal possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n2n-2=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nFor a \\(2025\\times2025\\) board the smallest number of rectangular tiles\nthat can be placed so that each row and each column contains exactly one\nuncovered unit square is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
