t part of the row.\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) tile covering the whole right part of the row.\n\nThe families \\(\\{L_i\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(\\{R_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consist of\n\\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.  \nThey are pairwise disjoint because each \\(L_i\\) lies strictly left of the diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) and each \\(R_i\\) lies strictly right of it; rows are distinct, so no two rectangles intersect.  \nEvery cell that is not on the diagonal belongs either to some \\(L_i\\) (if it lies left of the diagonal in its row) or to some \\(R_i\\) (if it lies right of the diagonal).  Hence the union of the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles is exactly \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}^2\\setminus U\\).\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Minimality\n\nCorollary\u202f1 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Answer\n\nThe minimum number of rectangular tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
