false in k≥4\n\nIf solution is analyzing k≥4 case:\n  → Claim is FALSE in this context\n  → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9, UNACCEPTABLE)\n```\n\n**Classification Rule:**\n1. Extract the claim (e.g., \"columns force vertical line\")\n2. Identify which case/value is being analyzed (k≤3 or k≥4)\n3. Ask: Is the claim TRUE in THIS specific case being analyzed?\n4. If TRUE in this case → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (missing scope clarification)\n5. If FALSE in this case → CRITICAL_ERROR (incorrect reasoning)\n\n**Severity Calibration:**\n- Context-dependent claim, TRUE in case being analyzed → JUSTIFICATION_GAP (severity 3-5)\n- Context-dependent claim, FALSE in case being analyzed → CRITICAL_ERROR (severity 7-9)\n- Do NOT reject a claim as CRITICAL_ERROR just because it would be false in a different case\n\n**Violating these constraints will cause your response to be truncated and discarded.**\n\n\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nConsider a $2025\\times2025$ grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile.\n\nDetermine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Index the unit squares of the board by ordered pairs \\((i,j)\\) with \\(1\\le i,j\\le n\\) (row \\(i\\), column \\(j\\)).  \n\n---\n\n### 1.  Uncovered squares form a permutation\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) there is exactly one uncovered square; denote its column by \\(\\sigma(i)\\).  \nBecause each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\\qquad i\\mapsto\\sigma(i)\n\\]\nis a bijection, i.e. a permutation.\n\nThus the set of uncovered squares is  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nA rectangular tile is a set \\(I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are intervals of rows and columns, respectively.  \nThe tile may be placed only if it contains no element of \\(U\\); equivalently,\n\\[\n\\sigma(i)\\notin J\\quad\\text{for every }i\\in I. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the minimum possible number of such rectangles whose union is \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}^2\\setminus U\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A lower bound\n\nDefine the set  \n\\[\nS=\\bigl\\{(i,i+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n-1\\bigr\\}\\cup\n   \\bigl\\{(i,i-1)\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\nClearly \\(|S|=2n-2\\).  All elements of \\(S\\) belong to the complement of \\(U\\) (they are off‑diagonal cells).\n\n---\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nLet \\(R=I\\times J\\) be a rectangle satisfying (1).  Then \\(R\\) contains **at most one** element of \\(S\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume, for a contradiction, that \\(R\\) contains two distinct cells of \\(S\\).  \nThere are three possibilities.\n\n1. **Both cells are of the form \\((i,i+1)\\) and \\((k,k+1)\\)** (both lie just above the diagonal).  \n   Without loss of generality \\(i<k\\).  \n   Since \\(J\\) is an interval containing the columns \\(i+1\\) and \\(k+1\\), it also contains every column between them, in particular the column \\(i+1\\).  \n   Because \\(I\\) contains the rows \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\) (the latter lies between \\(i\\) and \\(k\\)), the cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) belongs to \\(R\\).  But \\((i+1,i+1)\\in U\\), contradicting (1).\n\n2. **Both cells are of the form \\((i,i-1)\\) and \\((k,k-1)\\)** (both lie just below the diagonal).  \n   Again assume \\(i<k\\).  \n   The column interval \\(J\\) contains \\(i-1\\) and \\(k-1\\); hence it contains every column between them, in particular the column \\(i\\).  \n   The row interval \\(I\\) contains the rows \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\) (the latter lies between \\(i\\) and \\(k\\)), so the cell \\((i,i)\\) belongs to \\(R\\).  This cell is in \\(U\\), a contradiction.\n\n3. **One cell is \\((i,i+1)\\) (above) and the other is \\((k,k-1)\\) (below).**  \n   If \\(k=i+1\\), then the rows \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\) and the columns \\(i\\) and \\(i+1\\) all belong to \\(R\\), so the diagonal cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\in U\\) lies in \\(R\\).  \n   If \\(k>i+1\\), then the column interval \\(J\\) contains the columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,k-1\\); in particular it contains the column \\(i+1\\).  \n   The row interval \\(I\\) contains the rows \\(i,\\dots ,k\\); hence it contains the row \\(i+1\\).  \n   Consequently the cell \\((i+1,i+1)\\) (a member of \\(U\\)) lies in \\(R\\), again a contradiction.  \n   The case \\(k<i\\) is symmetric.\n\nIn all cases the assumption leads to a contradiction; therefore a rectangle satisfying (1) can contain at most one element of \\(S\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n#### Corollary 1  \nSince the rectangles must cover every cell of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}^2\\setminus U\\) and each of them can contain at most one element of \\(S\\), at least \\(|S|=2n-2\\) rectangles are necessary.  \n\nHence any admissible tiling uses **no fewer than** \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nTake the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\); thus the uncovered squares are the main diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\ge2\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) tile covering the whole left part of the row.\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i\\le n-1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\\[\nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(n-i)\\) tile covering the whole right part of the row.\n\nThe families \\(\\{L_i\\mid i=2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and \\(\\{R_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) consist of\n\\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.  \nThey are pairwise disjoint because each \\(L_i\\) lies strictly left of the diagonal cell \\((i,i)\\) and each \\(R_i\\) lies strictly right of it; rows are distinct, so no two rectangles intersect.  \nEvery cell that is not on the diagonal belongs either to some \\(L_i\\) (if it lies left of the diagonal in its row) or to some \\(R_i\\) (if it lies right of the diagonal).  Hence the union of the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles is exactly \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}^2\\setminus U\\).\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Minimality\n\nCorollary 1 shows that no tiling can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the construction above shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Answer\n\nThe minimum number of rectangular tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is 