ve exactly one uncovered cell? Yes, column j has uncovered cell (j,j). So this construction works.\n\nNow the lower bound: they define S = set of cells (i,i+1) and (i,i-1). That's 2n-2 cells. They claim each rectangle can contain at most one element of S. Let's check: Suppose we have a rectangle I x J that does not intersect any uncovered cell (i.e., sigma(i) not in J for all i in I). They claim that such a rectangle can contain at most one element of S. The proof uses contradictions: if rectangle contains two elements of S, they derive that rectangle must contain a diagonal cell (i,i) which is uncovered, violating condition. The proof seems plausible. But we need to verify if there is any subtlety: The rectangle may be larger than 1x something, but they consider intervals I and J. They assume that if rectangle contains (i,i+1) and (k,k+1), then I includes rows i and k, J includes columns i+1 and k+1. Since intervals, they include all rows between i and k and all columns between i+1 and k+1. Then they argue that (i+1,i+1) is inside rectangle. Indeed, if i<k, then row i+1 is between i and k, column i+1 is between i+1 and k+1. So (i+1,i+1) is inside rectangle. That cell is uncovered under identity sigma. But the rectangle is not allowed to contain any uncovered cell. So contradiction. Similarly for other cases. So the lemma holds for identity sigma? Wait, the lemma is for any sigma? The condition (1) is sigma(i) not in J for all i in I. So if rectangle contains (i,i+1) and (k,k+1), then we need to ensure that (i+1,i+1) is uncovered. But (i+1,i+1) is uncovered only if sigma(i+1) = i+1. But sigma is arbitrary permutation. However, the lemma is used to get lower bound for any sigma. Let's examine: The lemma statement: Let R = I x J be a rectangle satisfying (1). Then R contains at most one element of S. The proof uses the fact that (i+1,i+1) belongs to U. But U is defined as { (i, sigma(i)) }. So (i+1,i+1) belongs to U only if sigma(i+1) = i+1. But sigma is arbitrary; we