ometry of the grid, it does not affect the number of tiles needed.  \nThus we may assume without loss of generality that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Description of a tile  \n\nA tile is a rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines; therefore it is determined by a contiguous interval of rows \\([r_1,r_2]\\) and a contiguous interval of columns \\([c_1,c_2]\\) with  \n\n\\[\n1\\le r_1\\le r_2\\le n,\\qquad 1\\le c_1\\le c_2\\le n. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe tile covers precisely the squares  \n\n\\[\n\\{(i,j)\\mid r_1\\le i\\le r_2,\\;c_1\\le j\\le c_2\\}. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIf a tile contained a diagonal square \\((k,k)\\), then \\(k\\) would belong simultaneously to the row\u2011interval and the column\u2011interval, i.e. \\(r_1\\le k\\le r_2\\) and \\(c_1\\le k\\le c_2\\).  \nTo avoid covering any square of \\(U\\) we must have  \n\n\\[\n\\{r_1,\\dots ,r_2\\}\\cap\\{c_1,\\dots ,c_2\\}=\\varnothing . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause the two sets in (5) are intervals of integers, (5) forces one of the following:\n\n* \\(r_2<c_1\\) \u2013 the rectangle lies **strictly above** the main diagonal; all its squares satisfy \\(i<j\\).\n* \\(c_2<r_1\\) \u2013 the rectangle lies **strictly below** the main diagonal; all its squares satisfy \\(i>j\\).\n\nConsequently every tile belongs entirely to the set  \n\n\\[\nU^+=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}\\quad\\text{or}\\quad U^-=\\{(i,j)\\mid i>j\\}. \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus the problem separates into two independent subproblems: covering \\(U^+\\) and covering \\(U^-\\) by rectangles of the respective type.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound for covering \\(U^+\\)\n\nConsider the set of squares on the first super\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,i+1)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subseteq U^+. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nLet a rectangle \\(R\\) lie above the diagonal with row\u2011interval \\([a,b]\\) and column\u2011interval \\([c,d]\\) (so \\(b<c\\)).  \nIf \\((i,i+1)\\in S\\) belongs to \\(R\\), then  \n\n\\[\na\\le i\\le b,\\qquad c\\le i+1\\le d. \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b<c\\) we have \\(b\\le i\\) (otherwise \\(b\\ge c\\) would contradict \\(b<c\\)).  \nHence the row\u2011interval of \\(R\\) ends at a row \\(\\le i\\); consequently \\(R\\) cannot contain any square \\((j,j+1)\\) with \\(j>i\\).  \nThus **each rectangle above the diagonal contains at most one element of \\(S\\).**\n\nBecause \\(|S|=n-1\\), covering all squares of \\(U^+\\) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles lying above the diagonal.\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound for covering \\(U^-\\)\n\nBy symmetry, consider the first sub\u2011diagonal  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{(i+1,i)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\subseteq U^- . \\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nA rectangle below the diagonal has column\u2011interval ending before its row\u2011interval starts; an argument identical to that in \u00a73 shows that each such rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(T\\).  \nHence at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all squares of \\(U^-\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Combined lower bound  \n\nAny admissible tiling must contain at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 \\tag{10}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal as in (2).  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define  \n\n* a **horizontal** tile  \n  \\[\n  H_i=\\bigl(\\{i\\},\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\}\\bigr), \n  \\]  \n  i.e. the rectangle consisting of row \\(i\\) and columns \\(i+1,\\dots ,n\\) (size \\(1\\times (n-i)\\));\n\n* a **vertical** tile  \n  \\[\n  V_i=\\bigl(\\{i+1,i+2,\\dots ,n\\},\\{i\\}\\bigr), \n  \\]  \n  i.e. the rectangle consisting of column \\(i\\) and rows \\(i+1,\\dots ,n\\) (size \\((n-i)\\times 1\\)).\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{T}=\\{H_i,V_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\n\\]  \n\ncontains exactly \\(2(n-1)\\) rectangles.  \n\n*Disjointness.*  \nTwo distinct horizontal tiles lie in different rows, so they are disjoint.  \nTwo distinct vertical tiles lie in different columns, so they are disjoint.  \nA horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) contains squares \\((i,j)\\) with \\(j>i\\); a vertical tile \\(V_k\\) contains squares \\((j,k)\\) with \\(j>k\\).  \nIf \\(i\\neq k\\) then a square cannot simultaneously satisfy \\((i,j)=(j,k)\\); hence \\(H_i\\cap V_k=\\varnothing\\).  \nThus the tiles in \\(\\mathcal{T}\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((i,j)\\) be any off\u2011diagonal square.\n\n- If \\(i<j\\), then \\((i,j)\\) belongs to the horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) because its row is \\(i\\) and its column \\(j\\) lies in \\(\\{i+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n- If \\(i>j\\), then \\((i,j)\\) belongs to the vertical tile \\(V_j\\) because its column is \\(j\\) and its row \\(i\\) lies in \\(\\{j+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\nHence every square of \\(U^+\\cup U^-\\) is covered exactly once, while no tile contains a diagonal square.  \n\nTherefore a tiling with precisely \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 7.  Minimality  \n\nSection\u202f3 gave a lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and Section\u202f6 provides a tiling attaining this bound.  \nConsequently the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
