1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow \\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, the values \\(p(1),\\dots ,p(n)\\) are all distinct; hence \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  The set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nwill be called the *uncovered set*.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  An explicit tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); thus \\(U\\) is the main diagonal \\(\\{(i,i)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) define the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\mathrm{vert}}_{j}\n   =\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\((j-1)\\times1\\) rectangle.\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n\\) define the horizontal tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\mathrm{hor}}_{i}\n   =\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\nwhich is a \\(1\\times(i-1)\\) rectangle.\n\n*Disjointness.*  \nIf a cell \\((i,j)\\) satisfies \\(i<j\\) it belongs to the vertical tile \\(T^{\\mathrm{vert}}_{j}\\); if \\(i>j\\) it belongs to the horizontal tile \\(T^{\\mathrm{hor}}_{i}\\).  The case \\(i=j\\) never occurs because those cells are uncovered.  Hence no two tiles share a cell.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery off‑diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) is covered exactly once, as argued above.  Thus the collection  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T\n   =\\{T^{\\mathrm{vert}}_{j}\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\n     \\cup\\{T^{\\mathrm{hor}}_{i}\\mid 2\\le i\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncovers all squares of the board except the diagonal squares, and each row and column has exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one).  \n\nThe number of tiles in \\(\\mathcal T\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le 2n-2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound via induction  \n\nWe prove that any admissible tiling must use at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n**Base case \\(n=1\\).**  \nThe single cell must be uncovered, so no tile is placed; thus \\(f(1)=0=2\\cdot1-2\\).\n\n**Inductive hypothesis.**  \nAssume for some \\(k\\ge1\\) that every admissible tiling of a \\(k\\times k\\) board uses at least \\(2k-2\\) tiles, i.e. \\(f(k)\\ge 2k-2\\).\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nConsider an admissible tiling of a \\((k+1)\\times(k+1)\\) board.  \nLet the uncovered square in the last column be \\((r,k+1)\\); such a row \\(r\\) exists and is unique because each column contains exactly one uncovered square.\n\nRemove row \\(r\\) and column \\(k+1\\).  The remaining board is a \\(k\\times k\\) grid.  For every remaining row \\(i\\neq r\\) the uncovered square is still \\((i,p(i))\\) with \\(p(i)\\le k\\); for every remaining column \\(j\\le k\\) the uncovered square is \\((p^{-1}(j),j)\\).  Hence the reduced board also satisfies the “one uncovered per row and column’’ condition, and therefore needs at least \\(f(k)\\) tiles.\n\nNow examine the cells that were deleted:\n\n\\[\nH=\\{(r,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le k\\},\\qquad\nV=\\{(i,k+1)\\mid 1\\le i\\le r-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(H\\) is the horizontal segment of row \\(r\\) to the left of the uncovered cell, and \\(V\\) is the vertical segment of column \\(k+1\\) above the uncovered cell.  The union \\(H\\cup V\\) is an “L’’ shape whose corner \\((r,k+1)\\) is uncovered.\n\n**Lemma 1.** *No single rectangular tile can contain a cell of \\(H\\) and a cell of \\(V\\) simultaneously.*  \n\n*Proof.*  Suppose a rectangle \\(R\\) contains \\((r,j)\\in H\\) and \\((i,k+1)\\in V\\) with \\(i<r\\) and \\(j<k+1\\).  Since rectangles are convex in the grid sense, \\(R\\) must contain all cells whose row lies between \\(i\\) and \\(r\\) and whose column lies between \\(j\\) and \\(k+1\\); in particular it contains the corner cell \\((r,k+1)\\).  This cell is uncovered, contradicting the definition of a tile.  ∎\n\nConsequently any tile covering a cell of \\(H\\) is disjoint from all tiles covering cells of \\(V\\).  Since every cell of \\(H\\cup V\\) must be covered, at least one tile is needed for \\(H\\) and at least one tile for \\(V\\); thus at least two tiles intersect the removed row or column.  Moreover, a tile cannot intersect both the removed row and the removed column, for otherwise it would contain the uncovered corner \\((r,k+1)\\), violating Lemma 1.  Hence the two tiles are distinct.\n\nLet \\(t\\) be the total number of tiles in the original tiling.  Removing the tiles that intersect row \\(r\\) or column \\(k+1\\) leaves a tiling of the reduced \\(k\\times k\\) board, which uses at most \\(t-2\\) tiles.  Therefore\n\n\\[\nt-2\\ge f(k)\\ge 2k-2,\n\\]\n\nand consequently\n\n\\[\nt\\ge (2k-2)+2 = 2(k+1)-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(f(k+1)\\ge 2(k+1)-2\\), completing the inductive step.\n\nBy induction we have proved\n\n\\[\nf(n)\\ge 2n-2\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge1 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Determination of the minimum  \n\nFrom (3) and (4) we obtain the exact value\n\n\\[\nf(n)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nFor the specific board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\nf(2025)=2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nBefore finalizing, verify that:\n\n* All mathematical statements are expressed in TeX.\n* The three required sections appear in the prescribed order with exact headings.\n* The induction argument correctly handles tiles intersecting the removed row or column, using Lemma 1 to guarantee at least two such tiles.\n* No step relies on unproved intuition; every claim is justified.\n* The construction indeed uses \\(2n-2\\) tiles and covers every off‑diagonal cell exactly once.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check constructions: Explicit verification for p=3 provided ✓\n3. Decision: Answer correct → Classify as **Justification Gap**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"\n    *   **Issue:** Justification Gap - The solution should explicitly state \"since p>3 is prime, it's not divisible by 3, so p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\" for complete rigor. The claim is mathematically correct and the reasoning is sound, but lacks an explicit justification step. This is a presentation issue, not a mathematical error.\n\n**WRONG Classification (don't do this):**\n*   ~~**Location:** \"p≡1 or 2 (mod 3)\"~~\n    *   ~~**Issue:** Critical Error - This claim needs proof.~~ ❌ WRONG - This would be hypercritical; the claim is a standard fact about primes and the overall logic is valid.\n\n---\n\n**Example 2: Critical Error (truly invalid)**\n*This example shows a fundamental mathematical error.*\n\nProblem: \"Prove that √2 is irrational.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"I tried to express √2 as a fraction p/q for many different integers p and q, but I couldn't find any that work. After testing hundreds of fractions, I conclude that √2 cannot be expressed as a fraction. Therefore √2 is irrational.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: √2 is irrational ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check reasoning method: \"I tried many cases and failed\" ✗ INVALID (falls under EXCEPTION)\n3. Decision: Invalid reasoning → Classify as **Critical Error**\n\n**Correct Classification:**\n*   **Location:** \"I tried to express √2 as a fraction p/q for many different integers p and q, but I couldn't find any\"\n    *   **Issue:** Critical Error - Failure to find a counterexample is not a proof of a universal statement. The solution provides no rigorous argument (no proof by contradiction, no algebraic reasoning, no number theory). This falls under the IMPORTANT EXCEPTION in the decision rule: completely invalid reasoning even with correct answer.\n\n---\n\n**Example 3: Context-Dependent Claim (Justification Gap, NOT Critical Error)**\nProblem: \"Prove that if m is an even integer, then m² is divisible by 4.\"\nSolution excerpt: \"Let m=2j. Then m²=4j². Now for j even: m²=16r² since j is an integer from the first case.\"\n**Applying the Decision Rule:** 1. Answer: m² divisible by 4 ✓ CORRECT | 2. Method: Algebraic manipulation ✓ VALID | 3. Claim: \"j is integer from first case\" - TRUE from setup, but reference unclear\n**Correct Classification:** \"j is integer from first case\" → Justification Gap (4-5) - Claim IS TRUE (follows from m=2j), but \"first case\" reference imprecise. Missing cross-reference clarity, NOT provably false claim.\n**WRONG:** ~~Critical Error - claim FALSE~~ ❌ WRONG - \"j is integer\" IS TRUE (from m=2j with m∈ℤ), just reference imprecise. Missing explicit reference = JUSTIFICATION_GAP (4-5), NOT CRITICAL_ERROR (8-9).\n**CRITICAL RULE:** Context-dependent claims that are TRUE in context (even if reference imprecise) are JUSTIFICATION_GAP. Only CRITICAL_ERROR if claim provably FALSE or makes explicit universal claim that doesn't hold.\n\n---\n\n**Example 4: Count+Target Specification (Justification Gap, NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows how to classify construction claims with count and target.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that 2^p + p^2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt (p=3 construction): \"**p=3:** Computing 2³ + 3² = 8 + 9 = 17, which is prime.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p∈{2,3} ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check construction claim: \"Computing 2³ + 3² = 8 + 