"**### Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have found a complete solution. The minimum number of tiles required is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Reformulation.**  \n   The uncovered squares must form a permutation matrix: there is a bijection \\(f:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (with \\(n=2025\\)) such that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,f(i))\\). All other unit squares must be tiled by axis\u2011parallel rectangles (tiles) that do not contain any of the \\(n\\) forbidden squares.\n\n2. **Upper bound \\(2n-2\\).**  \n   Sort the rows by increasing values of \\(f\\):  \n   \\[\n   f(i_1)<f(i_2)<\\dots <f(i_n).\n   \\]\n   For each \\(k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) define two rectangles  \n\n   \\[\n   R_k=\\bigl\\{i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\bigr\\}\\times\\bigl\\{f(i_k)+1,\\dots ,n\\bigr\\},\n   \\qquad \n   S_k=\\bigl\\{i_{k+1},\\dots ,i_n\\bigr\\}\\times\\bigl\\{1,\\dots ,f(i_{k+1})-1\\bigr\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(R_k\\) lies completely to the right of all holes in its rows, each \\(S_k\\) lies completely to the left of all holes in its rows; together the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles partition the whole board except the \\(n\\) holes.\n\n3. **Lower bound \\(2n-2\\).**  \n   Consider the \u201cleft\u201d region  \n   \\[\n   L=\\{(i,j):j<f(i)\\},\n   \\]\n   and the \u201cright\u201d region  \n   \\[\n   U=\\{(i,j):j>f(i)\\}.\n   \\]\n   In \\(L\\) the rightmost square of row \\(i\\) is \\((i,r_i)\\) with \\(r_i=f(i)-1\\).  \n   The numbers \\(r_1,\\dots ,r_n\\) are a permutation of \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); consequently there are exactly \\(n-1\\) rows with a rightmost square (the row with \\(r_i=0\\) has none).\n\n   *Lemma.* A rectangle contained in \\(L\\) can contain at most one of these rightmost squares.  \n   *Proof.* Let a rectangle cover rows \\(I=[a,b]\\) and columns \\([c,d]\\). Since it stays inside \\(L\\) we have \\(d\\le\\min_{i\\in I}r_i\\). Hence a rightmost square \\((i,r_i)\\) can belong to the rectangle only when \\(r_i=\\min_{i\\in I}r_i\\); at most one such row exists because the \\(r_i\\) are distinct.\n\n   Therefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all rightmost squares of \\(L\\); consequently any tiling of \\(L\\) uses at least \\(n-1\\) tiles.\n\n   The same argument applied to the right region \\(U\\) (using the leftmost squares \\((i,f(i)+1)\\)) shows that at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are necessary for \\(U\\).\n\n   Since the two regions are disjoint, any tiling of the whole board needs at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   rectangles.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The construction above uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the lower\u2011bound argument shows that no tiling can use fewer. Hence the minimum number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025,\n\\qquad \n\\mathcal G=\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of unit squares of the \\(n\\times n\\) board.  \nA tiling consists of axis\u2011parallel rectangles whose interiors are disjoint and whose union is \\(\\mathcal G\\setminus H\\), where  \n\n\\[\nH=\\{(i,f(i))\\mid i\\in\\mathcal G\\}\n\\]\n\nis the set of uncovered squares.  \nBecause each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the map  \n\n\\[\nf:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis a permutation.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound \\(2n-2\\)\n\nOrder the rows so that the values of \\(f\\) are increasing:\n\n\\[\nf(i_1)<f(i_2)<\\dots <f(i_n).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define two rectangles\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nR_k&=\\bigl\\{i_1,i_2,\\dots ,i_k\\bigr\\}\\times\n      \\bigl\\{\\,f(i_k)+1,\\;f(i_k)+2,\\dots ,n\\,\\bigr\\},\\\\[2mm]\nS_k&=\\bigl\\{i_{k+1},i_{k+2},\\dots ,i_n\\bigr\\}\\times\n      \\bigl\\{\\,1,2,\\dots ,f(i_{k+1})-1\\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*Verification that the rectangles are legal.*  \nConsider \\(R_k\\).  By (1) the largest forbidden column among the rows\n\\(\\{i_1,\\dots ,i_k\\}\\) is \\(f(i_k)\\); all columns strictly larger than\n\\(f(i_k)\\) are therefore free of holes in those rows, so \\(R_k\\subseteq\n\\mathcal G\\setminus H\\).  An analogous argument shows \\(S_k\\subseteq\n\\mathcal G\\setminus H\\).\n\n*Disjointness and completeness.*  \nThe sets \\(\\{R_k\\}_{k=1}^{n-1}\\) are pairwise disjoint because their\ncolumn intervals are nested decreasingly:\n\\(R_{k+1}\\) uses columns \\(>f(i_{k+1})\\), which are a proper subset of the\ncolumns used by \\(R_k\\).  The same holds for the family \\(\\{S_k\\}\\).\nMoreover, every square \\((i,j)\\notin H\\) belongs either to the left of\nits hole (\\(j<f(i)\\)) or to the right of its hole (\\(j>f(i)\\)).  If\n\\(j>f(i)\\) then, letting \\(k\\) be the index with \\(i=i_k\\), we have\n\\(j>f(i)=f(i_k)\\) and consequently \\((i,j)\\in R_k\\).  If \\(j<f(i)\\) then,\nwith the same notation, \\(j<f(i)=f(i_k)\\) and \\((i,j)\\in S_{k-1}\\).\nThus the \\(2n-2\\) rectangles in (2) partition \\(\\mathcal G\\setminus H\\).\n\nHence a tiling with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles always exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound \\(2n-2\\)\n\nDefine the *left* and *right* regions\n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<f(i)\\},\\qquad \nU=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>f(i)\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBoth regions are disjoint and together equal \\(\\mathcal G\\setminus H\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 1 (rightmost squares of \\(L\\))\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) put  \n\n\\[\nr_i:=f(i)-1\\qquad (0\\le r_i\\le n-1).\n\\]\n\nIf \\(r_i>0\\) the square \\((i,r_i)\\) is the rightmost square of row \\(i\\) in\n\\(L\\).  Because \\(f\\) is a permutation, the numbers\n\\(\\{r_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); hence\nexactly \\(n-1\\) rows possess a rightmost square.\n\n*Claim.*  Any axis\u2011parallel rectangle contained in \\(L\\) contains at most\none rightmost square.\n\n*Proof.*  Let a rectangle cover rows \\(I=[a,b]\\) and columns\n\\([c,d]\\).  Since it stays inside \\(L\\), for every \\(i\\in I\\) we must have\n\\(d\\le r_i\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\nd\\le\\min_{i\\in I}r_i. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA rightmost square \\((i,r_i)\\) can belong to the rectangle only when\n\\(r_i=d\\), i.e. when \\(r_i=\\min_{i\\in I}r_i\\).  Because the values\n\\(r_i\\) are all distinct, at most one index \\(i\\) satisfies this equality.\n\u220e\n\nTherefore covering all rightmost squares of \\(L\\) requires at least\n\\(n-1\\) rectangles.  Since every rectangle covering a part of \\(L\\) can be\nrefined (by cutting away unused rows or columns) into a rectangle that\nstill lies in \\(L\\), the same lower bound holds for any tiling of \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Lemma 2 (leftmost squares of \\(U\\))\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(f(i)<n\\) define the leftmost square of \\(U\\) as\n\\((i,\\ell_i)\\) where \\(\\ell_i:=f(i)+1\\).  Exactly \\(n-1\\) rows have such a\nsquare (the unique row with \\(f(i)=n\\) has none).\n\n*Claim.*  Any rectangle contained in \\(U\\) contains at most one of the\nsquares \\((i,\\ell_i)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  Let a rectangle cover rows \\(I=[a,b]\\) and columns \\([c,d]\\).\nBecause it stays inside \\(U\\) we must have \\(c>\\max_{i\\in I}f(i)\\); equivalently  \n\n\\[\nc\\ge\\max_{i\\in I}f(i)+1. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA leftmost square \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) can belong to the rectangle only when\n\\(\\ell_i=c\\), i.e. when \\(f(i)=\\max_{i\\in I}f(i)\\).  Again the values\n\\(f(i)\\) are distinct, so at most one such row exists. \u220e\n\nThus any tiling of \\(U\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### Completion of the lower bound\n\nThe two regions \\(L\\) and \\(U\\) are disjoint, so a tiling of the whole\nboard uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Equality of the bounds\n\nFrom Sections\u202f1 and\u202f2 we have shown\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2n-2\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}\\;\\le\\;2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the concrete size \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus Matilda needs at least, and can achieve, exactly 4048 rectangular\ntiles.**"
