squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n   *Left tiles.* For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n   \\[\n   L_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n   In every row \\(i>j\\) the hole is at column \\(i>j\\), so column \\(j\\) lies left of the hole; thus \\(L_j\\) contains no hole.\n\n   *Right tiles.* For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n   \\[\n   R_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n   \\]\n   In every row \\(i<j\\) the hole is at column \\(i<j\\), so column \\(j\\) lies right of the hole; thus \\(R_j\\) contains no hole.\n\n   The families \\(\\{L_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{R_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) consist of \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) pairwise disjoint rectangles.  \n   Every non\u2011diagonal cell \\((i,j)\\) satisfies either \\(i>j\\) (hence belongs to \\(L_j\\)) or \\(i<j\\) (hence belongs to \\(R_j\\)). Consequently all squares except the diagonal are covered exactly once, and each row and column has precisely one uncovered square (the diagonal one).\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) is attainable, so it is the minimum possible number of tiles. For \\(n=2025\\),\n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the required minimum is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  Denote the set of unit squares of the board by  \n\n\\[\nB=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\((i,j)\\) is the square in row \\(i\\) and column \\(j\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nSince each row and each column must contain exactly one uncovered square, the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix.  \nThus there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the unique uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \nConsequently the uncovered squares are precisely  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<p(i)\\},\\qquad \nR_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_i\\) consists of the squares left of the hole in row \\(i\\), and \\(R_i\\) those to its right.  The sets \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{R_i\\}\\) are disjoint and together cover all squares of \\(B\\setminus U\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Tiles are either left or right  \n\nConsider a tile \\(T\\), i.e. an axis\u2011parallel rectangle whose sides lie on grid lines.  Write  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{(i,j)\\mid a\\le i\\le b,\\;c\\le j\\le d\\},\n\\]\n\nwith integers \\(1\\le a\\le b\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le c\\le d\\le n\\).  \nFor any row \\(i\\) with \\(a\\le i\\le b\\) the tile occupies the columns \\(c,\\dots ,d\\).  Since the hole in that row is \\((i,p(i))\\) and the tile may not cover a hole, we must have either  \n\n\\[\nd<p(i)\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad c>p(i).\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nIf (1) holds with the first inequality for some row \\(i\\) and with the second inequality for another row \\(i'\\), then we would have  \n\n\\[\nc>p(i')\\quad\\text{and}\\quad d<p(i).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(c\\le d\\), this would imply \\(p(i')<c\\le d<p(i)\\), i.e. \\(p(i')<p(i)\\).  While this is not contradictory, note that the column interval \\([c,d]\\) would then lie strictly to the left of the hole in row \\(i\\) and strictly to the right of the hole in row \\(i'\\).  Consequently the tile would contain column \\(c\\) (or \\(d\\)) which is on opposite sides of the two holes, but this does not violate any condition.  However, the crucial observation is that **if a tile contains a column \\(j\\) for which there exists a row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)=j+1\\), then the tile cannot extend to column \\(j+1\\)**, because column \\(j+1\\) contains a hole in that row.  Hence the tile\u2019s column interval must be entirely on one side of all holes it meets.  In particular, a tile cannot be left of the hole in some rows and right of the hole in others; otherwise its column interval would have to cross a column that is a hole for some row it covers, which is impossible.  Therefore every tile satisfies one of the following:\n\n* **Left tile:** for all rows \\(i\\) it meets, \\(d<p(i)\\); i.e. the whole tile lies in \\(\\bigcup_i L_i\\).\n* **Right tile:** for all rows \\(i\\) it meets, \\(c>p(i)\\); i.e. the whole tile lies in \\(\\bigcup_i R_i\\).\n\nThus the collection of tiles splits into left tiles and right tiles.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lower bound for the number of left tiles  \n\nFor each column index \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) let  \n\n\\[\ni_j\\;:=\\;\\text{the unique row satisfying }p(i_j)=j+1.\n\\]\n\nSuch a row exists because \\(p\\) is a permutation.  The square \\((i_j,j)\\) belongs to \\(L_{i_j}\\) (it is left of the hole \\((i_j,j+1)\\)).  Let \\(T_j\\) be the left tile that covers \\((i_j,j)\\) (such a tile must exist because every square must be covered).  \n\nSince column \\(j+1\\) contains the hole \\((i_j,j+1)\\), the tile \\(T_j\\) cannot contain column \\(j+1\\); otherwise it would cover a hole.  Consequently the rightmost column of \\(T_j\\) is at most \\(j\\).  Because \\(T_j\\) does contain column \\(j\\), its rightmost column is exactly \\(j\\).  \n\nThus each index \\(j\\) determines a left tile whose rightmost column is \\(j\\).  Different values of \\(j\\) give different rightmost columns, so the tiles \\(\\{T_j\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\) are distinct.  Hence any tiling must contain at least \\(n-1\\) left tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{left tiles}\\}\\ge n-1.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound for the number of right tiles  \n\nThe argument is symmetric.  For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\ni'_j\\;:=\\;\\text{the unique row satisfying }p(i'_j)=j-1.\n\\]\n\nThe square \\((i'_j,j)\\) belongs to \\(R_{i'_j}\\) (it is right of the hole \\((i'_j,j-1)\\)).  Let \\(S_j\\) be the right tile covering \\((i'_j,j)\\).  \n\nSince column \\(j-1\\) contains the hole \\((i'_j,j-1)\\), the tile \\(S_j\\) cannot contain column \\(j-1\\); therefore its leftmost column is exactly \\(j\\).  Distinct values of \\(j\\) give distinct leftmost columns, so the tiles \\(\\{S_j\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\\) are all different.  Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{right tiles}\\}\\ge n-1.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Global lower bound  \n\nAdding (2) and (3) yields the universal lower bound\n\n\\[\n\\#\\{\\text{tiles}\\}\\;\\ge\\;(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Construction achieving the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation  \n\n\\[\np(i)=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle*\n\n\\[\nL_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j+1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn any row \\(i>j\\) the hole is at column \\(i>j\\); thus column \\(j\\) lies left of the hole, so \\(L_j\\) contains no uncovered square.  The rectangle \\(L_j\\) is a left tile whose rightmost column is \\(j\\).\n\n*For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle*\n\n\\[\nR_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le j-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIn any row \\(i<j\\) the hole is at column \\(i<j\\); thus column \\(j\\) lies right of the hole, so \\(R_j\\) contains no uncovered square.  The rectangle \\(R_j\\) is a right tile whose leftmost column is \\(j\\).\n\nThe families \\(\\{L_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{R_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) consist of \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) rectangles.  They are pairwise disjoint because each rectangle occupies a single column and a set of rows that does not intersect the row set of any other rectangle in the same column.  \n\nEvery non\u2011diagonal square \\((i,j)\\) satisfies either \\(i>j\\) or \\(i<j\\).  \nIf \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in L_j\\); if \\(i<j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in R_j\\).  Hence all squares of \\(B\\setminus U\\) are covered exactly once, while the diagonal squares remain uncovered.  Consequently each row and each column contains precisely one uncovered square, as required.\n\nThus the construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles, meeting the lower bound (4).\n\n---\n\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nThe minimal possible number of tiles equals the lower bound \\(2n-2\\).  For the given board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\nTherefore the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
