024=4048\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation\n\\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered, and a tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose\ninterior contains no element of \\(U\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Splitting the board\n\nDefine two disjoint subsets of the covered squares:\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\nThus \\(L\\) consists of the cells lying **left** of the uncovered square in its row,\nand \\(R\\) consists of the cells lying **right** of it.  \nClearly \\(L\\cup R\\) is the whole set of covered squares.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A lower bound for \\(|\\mathcal T_L|\\)\n\nConsider the left part \\(L\\).  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the permutation property guarantees a unique\nrow \\(i_j\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_j)=j+1\\).  \nThe cell \\((i_j,j)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) because \\(j<\\sigma(i_j)\\).\n\nSuppose a rectangle \\(R\\) of a tiling contains \\((i_j,j)\\).  \nIf \\(R\\) also contained column \\(j+1\\), then it would contain the uncovered cell\n\\((i_j,j+1)\\), which is forbidden.  \nHence the **rightmost column** of the rectangle that covers \\((i_j,j)\\) is at most \\(j\\).\n\nConsequently column \\(j\\) must be the rightmost column of **some** rectangle of the tiling.\nSince a rectangle has a unique rightmost column, distinct columns require distinct rectangles.\nThus any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles:\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_L|\\ge n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A lower bound for \\(|\\mathcal T_R|\\)\n\nThe right part \\(R\\) is symmetric.  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) there is a unique row \\(i'_j\\) with\n\\(\\sigma(i'_j)=j-1\\).  \nThe cell \\((i'_j,j)\\) lies in \\(R\\) because \\(j>\\sigma(i'_j)\\).\n\nIf a rectangle covering \\((i'_j,j)\\) extended to column \\(j-1\\), it would contain the\nuncovered cell \\((i'_j,j-1)\\).  Hence the **leftmost column** of the rectangle that\ncovers \\((i'_j,j)\\) is at least \\(j\\).  Therefore column \\(j\\) must be the leftmost\ncolumn of some rectangle of the tiling, and again distinct columns require distinct\nrectangles.  Hence\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_R|\\ge n-1. \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Global lower bound\n\nEvery tile belongs either to the left part or to the right part (it cannot cross an\nuncovered square).  From (1) and (2) we obtain\n\\[\n\\text{number of tiles}\\;\\; \\ge\\; |\\mathcal T_L|+|\\mathcal T_R|\\;\\ge\\;2(n-1). \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  A tiling attaining the bound\n\nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the vertical rectangle\n\\[\nT_j^{L}= \\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)>j\\}.\n\\]\nAll rows whose uncovered square lies to the right of column \\(j\\) are included,\nand no uncovered square lies inside \\(T_j^{L}\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the vertical rectangle\n\\[\nT_j^{R}= \\{(i,j)\\mid \\sigma(i)<j\\}.\n\\]\nAll rows whose uncovered square lies to the left of column \\(j\\) are included,\nand again no uncovered square lies inside \\(T_j^{R}\\).\n\n*Disjointness.*  \nFix a column \\(j\\).  The two row‑sets\n\\(\\{i\\mid\\sigma(i)>j\\}\\) and \\(\\{i\\mid\\sigma(i)<j\\}\\) are complementary\n(because \\(\\sigma(i)=j\\) for exactly one row), so \\(T_j^{L}\\) and \\(T_j^{R}\\) are\ndisjoint.  Different columns involve different column indices, hence all\n\\(T_j^{L}\\) and \\(T_j^{R}\\) are pairwise disjoint.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nEvery covered cell belongs either to the left of its row’s uncovered square\n(i.e. lies in \\(L\\)) or to the right (lies in \\(R\\)).  By construction it is then\ncontained in the appropriate vertical rectangle.  No uncovered cell is covered,\nbecause each vertical rectangle avoids the unique column \\(\\sigma(i)\\) of every\nrow it contains.\n\nThus the family\n\\[\n\\mathcal T=\\{T_j^{L}\\mid 1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{T_j^{R}\\mid 2\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\nis a valid tiling.  Its cardinality is\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2. \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Minimality\n\nInequality (3) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while construction\n(4) achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  Hence the bound is tight and the minimum\npossible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the given board \\(n=2025\\),\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]"
