4048\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a permutation matrix: there exists a permutation\n\\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered squares are precisely\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll other unit squares must be covered, and a tile is an axis‑parallel rectangle whose\ninterior contains no element of \\(U\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Splitting the board\n\nDefine two disjoint subsets of the covered squares:\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\nThus \\(L\\) consists of the cells lying **left** of the uncovered square in its row,\nand \\(R\\) consists of the cells lying **right** of it.  \nClearly \\(L\\cup R\\) is the whole set of covered squares.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  A lower bound for \\(|\\mathcal T_L|\\)\n\nConsider the left part \\(L\\).  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the permutation property guarantees a unique\nrow \\(i_j\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_j)=j+1\\).  \nThe cell \\((i_j,j)\\) belongs to \\(L\\) because \\(j<\\sigma(i_j)\\).\n\nSuppose a rectangle \\(R\\) of a tiling contains \\((i_j,j)\\).  \nIf \\(R\\) also contained column \\(j+1\\), then it would contain the uncovered cell\n\\((i_j,j+1)\\), which is forbidden.  \nHence the **rightmost column** of the rectangle that covers \\((i_j,j)\\) is at most \\(j\\).\n\nConsequently column \\(j\\) must be the rightmost column of **some** rectangle of the tiling.\nSince a rectangle has a unique rightmost column, distinct columns require distinct rectangles.\nThus any tiling of \\(L\\) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles:\n\\[\n|\\mathcal T_L|\\ge n-1. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A lower bound for \\(|\\mathcal T_R|\\)\n\nThe right part \\(R\\) is symmetric.  \nFor each column \\(j\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\