n{σ(i) | i ∈ I_{k,l}} - 1. Since all rows i in I_{k,l} have σ(i) > k, the min is at least k+1, so m_{k,l} ≥ k. The rectangle T_{k,l} = { (i,j) | i ∈ I_{k,l}, k ≤ j ≤ m_{k,l} } is a rectangle covering columns from k to m_{k,l} inclusive (or maybe inclusive? They wrote k ≤ j ≤ m_{k,l}, so inclusive). Since m_{k,l} = min σ(i) - 1, the rectangle stops just before the smallest uncovered column among rows in the interval. So it stays entirely left of all uncovered squares in that interval, thus inside L.\n\nNow they claim that the family of all such T_{k,l} for all k, l partitions L. Let's test: For any cell (i,j) ∈ L, we have j < σ(i). Set k = j. Then i ∈ S_k because σ(i) > k = j. Then i belongs to some interval I_{k,l} (the unique interval containing i). Then m_{k,l} = min_{i' ∈ I_{k,l}} σ(i') - 1. Since i ∈ I_{k,l}, we have σ(i) ≥ min σ(i') = m_{k,l}+1, so σ(i) ≥ m_{k,l}+1, thus j = k ≤ m_{k,l}. So (i,j) ∈ T_{k,l}. So coverage holds.\n\nDisjointness: For two distinct rectangles T_{k,l} and T_{k',l'}, either k ≠ k' (different leftmost column) or same k but different intervals I_{k,l} and I_{k,l'} are disjoint sets of rows, so rectangles don't intersect. So indeed they are disjoint.\n\nThus the construction partitions L. Counting: For each column k (1 ≤ k ≤ n-1), they claim exactly one new rectangle starts at column k. Let's examine: For each k, they define rectangles T_{k,l} for each interval I_{k,l}. But they claim that the total number of rectangles equals number of columns, i.e., n-1. How can that be? Because they claim that as we move from column k-1 to column k, we delete the row r_k = σ^{-1}(k). Deleting a row either removes an endpoint of an interval (so number of intervals stays same) or splits an interval into two (so number of intervals increases by one). In either case exactly one new rectangle starts at column k. Wait, that seems contradictory: If we delete a row, the number of intervals can increase by one (if the row was interior of an interval, splitting 