equal to the number of rows (or columns) minus 1? Let's examine.\n\nWe have a permutation sigma. The left region L = {(i,j) | j < sigma(i)}. That's the region below the permutation (if we think of rows as i and columns as j). Actually, it's the region to the left of each uncovered square in its row. So each row i has uncovered column sigma(i). The region left of that is columns 1,...,sigma(i)-1. So L is a shape that is monotone: as i increases, sigma(i) can go up or down arbitrarily. So L is not necessarily a Young diagram (i.e., monotone decreasing shape). But the shape L is a \"permutation diagram\" which is a set of cells left of the permutation points. Similarly, R is the region right of the permutation points.\n\nThe minimal number of rectangles needed to tile L is known to be equal to the number of columns that contain at least one cell of L (i.e., columns 1,...,n-1). Actually, the solution claims at least n-1 rectangles needed for L because each column c (1 to n-1) must be the rightmost column of at least one rectangle. They argue that the cell (r_c, c) where r_c is the row such that sigma(r_c) = c+1 cannot be covered by any rectangle that extends beyond column c, because that would include the uncovered cell at column c+1 in that row. So column c must be the rightmost column of some rectangle. Since each rectangle has a unique rightmost column, we need at least one rectangle per column c, giving n-1 rectangles. That seems valid.\n\nSimilarly for R, we need at least n-1 rectangles because each column c (2,...,n) must be the leftmost column of some rectangle? Actually they argue by symmetry.\n\nThus lower bound 2n-2 is valid.\n\nNow the construction: They propose a tiling of L using rectangles T_{k,\\ell} defined by intervals of rows S_k = {i | sigma(i) > k}. For each column k (1 <= k <= n-1), we consider rows where the uncovered column is to the right of column k. Then they partition S_k into maximal contiguous intervals I_{k,\\ell}. For each interval, define m_{k,\\ell} = min{sigma(i) | i in I_{k,\\ell}} - 1, i.e., the leftmost uncovered column among those rows minus 1. Then T_{k,\\ell} = { (i,j) | i in I_{k,\\ell}, k <= j <= m_{k,\\ell} }. This rectangle starts at column k and ends at column m_{k,\\ell}. Since m_{k,\\ell} < sigma(i) for all i in I_{k,\\ell}, the rectangle is inside L. They claim that these rectangles partition L. They also argue that the number of rectangles equals n-1 because each column k introduces exactly one new rectangle (starting at column k). That seems plausible: as we go from column k-1 to column k, we delete the row r_k = sigma^{-1}(k) from S_{k-1} to get S_k. Deleting a row either removes an endpoint of an interval (no new interval) or splits an interval into two (increase intervals by one). In either case exactly one new rectangle starts at column k. So total rectangles = number of columns = n-1.\n\nThus they have a construction achieving n-1 rectangles for L, and similarly for R, total 2n-2.\n\nThus answer appears correct.\n\nNow we need to verify the solution's reasoning uses valid methods. The solution uses combinatorial reasoning, case analysis, counting arguments, construction, intervals, etc. All are valid methods. So Level 2 passes.\n\nNow Level 3: Presentation quality. We need to check for any justification gaps or critical errors. The solution is fairly detailed. However, there may be some gaps: e.g., they claim \"the cell (r_c, c) cannot belong to a rectangle whose right side exceeds column c; hence column c must be the rightmost column of at least one rectangle of the tiling of L.\" This is a valid argument but they didn't explicitly prove that there is no rectangle that covers that cell and extends beyond column c. However, the reasoning is clear: if a rectangle covering (r_c, c) extended beyond column c, it would also cover (r_c, c+1) which is uncovered. So it's valid. The argument is sound.\n\nThe construction: They claim the family of rectangles partitions L. They provide coverage and disjointness arguments. The coverage argument: any cell (i,j) in L has j < sigma(i). Setting k=j, we have i in S_k, thus i belongs to a unique interval I_{k,\\ell}, and (i,j) in T_{k,\\ell}. That's fine.\n\nThe disjointness argument: two distinct rectangles have different leftmost columns or same leftmost column but correspond to different intervals of S_k; intervals are disjoint, so rectangles are disjoint. That's fine.\n\nCounting rectangles: They claim exactly one new rectangle starts at each column k. They argue that when we delete row r_k, either we remove an endpoint of an interval (no new interval) or split an interval into two (increase intervals by one). In either case exactly one new rectangle starts at column k. This is a bit subtle: if we remove an endpoint, the number of intervals stays the same, but they claim exactly one new rectangle starts at column k. Actually, they need to argue that each column k yields exactly one new rectangle regardless of whether intervals increase or not. The reasoning is plausible: the rectangle that starts at column k corresponds to the interval that contains row r_k? Wait, we need to examine.\n\nLet's examine the construction more concretely. For each column k, we consider S_k = rows where sigma(i) > k. So S_k includes all rows whose uncovered column is to the right of column k. For each maximal contiguous interval I_{k,\\ell} of S_k, we define a rectangle T_{k,\\ell} that starts at column k and ends at column m_{k,\\ell} = min sigma(i) - 1 among rows in the interval. So each interval yields a rectangle that starts at column k. So the number of rectangles that start at column k equals the number of intervals t_k of S_k. So total number of rectangles = sum_{k=1}^{n-1} t_k.\n\nNow they claim that sum_{k=1}^{n-1} t_k = n-1. They argue that as we go from column k-1 to column k, we delete the row r_k = sigma^{-1}(k). Deleting a row either removes an endpoint of an interval (the number of intervals stays the same) or splits an interval into two (the number of intervals increases by one). In either case exactly one new rectangle starts at column k. Wait, this seems contradictory: If the number of intervals stays the same, then t_k = t_{k-1} (no new interval). But they claim exactly one new rectangle starts at column k. Actually, they claim that each column k yields exactly one new rectangle, not that t_k = 1. The number of new rectangles starting at column k is the number of intervals in S_k, but they claim that the number of intervals t_k is exactly 1 for each k? That cannot be true in general. Let's examine.\n\nSuppose sigma is identity: sigma(i) = i. Then uncovered squares are diagonal. Then L = cells left of diagonal, i.e., lower triangular region (strictly left of diagonal). For each column k, S_k = rows i where sigma(i) > k, i.e., rows i > k. So S_k = {i | i > k} = {k+1, k+2, ..., n}. This is a contiguous interval (single interval). So t_k = 1 for all k. So total rectangles = n-1. That matches.\n\nNow suppose sigma is reverse identity: sigma(i) = n+1-i. Then uncovered squares are anti-diagonal. Then L = cells left of each uncovered square, i.e., for each row i, uncovered column is n+1-i, so left region includes columns 1,...,n-i. So L is shape of staircase from top-left to bottom-right? Actually, for row i, left region columns up to n-i. So L is a shape that is decreasing in row index: row 1 has n-1 cells, row 2 has n-2 cells, ..., row n-1 has 1 cell, row n has 0 cells. That's a Young diagram shape (staircase). For each column k, S_k = rows i where sigma(i) > k, i.e., n+1-i > k => i < n+1-k. So S_k = {i | i <= n - k}. That's also a contiguous interval (rows 1,...,n-k). So t_k = 1 for all k. So total rectangles = n-1. Good.\n\nNow consider a more complex permutation, e.g., sigma = (2,1,4,3,6,5,...). For each column k, S_k may have multiple intervals. For example, n=6, sigma = [2,1,4,3,6,5]. Then S_1 = rows where sigma(i) > 1: all rows except those with sigma(i)=1, which is