eeds at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n4. **Global lower bound.**  \n   Since every tile lies wholly in \\(L\\) or wholly in \\(R\\), the total number of tiles is at least  \n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n5. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Keep the diagonal holes.  \n   – For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     T_j^{\\,L}= \\{(i,j):j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n     which covers the whole part of column \\(j\\) below the diagonal.  \n   – For each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n     \\[\n     T_j^{\\,R}= \\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n     \\]\n     which covers the whole part of column \\(j\\) above the diagonal.  \n\n   The \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_j^{\\,L}\\}\\) are disjoint and together cover \\(L\\); the \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{T_j^{\\,R}\\}\\) are disjoint and together cover \\(R\\). No rectangle contains a diagonal cell, and the rectangles are pairwise disjoint. Hence we obtain exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n6. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction achieving it coincide, so the minimum number of tiles required for a \\(2025\\times2025\\) board is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048 .\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{4048}\n   \\]"
