id, need exactly one uncovered per row and column, so holes must be at positions (1,1) and (2,2) or (1,2) and (2,1). Let's consider diagonal holes (1,1) and (2,2). Then the remaining cells are (1,2) and (2,1). These cannot be in same rectangle because any rectangle covering both would include (1,1) or (2,2) (the diagonal). Actually rectangle covering (1,2) and (2,1) would be the whole 2x2 grid, which includes holes. So they must be separate rectangles, each covering a single cell. So number of tiles = 2. 2n-2 = 2. So matches. For n=3, diagonal holes at (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). The remaining cells are 6 cells: lower triangle L: (2,1),(3,1),(3,2); upper triangle R: (1,2),(1,3),(2,3). The solution's construction uses vertical rectangles per column: For L, columns j=1..2: T_j^L = {(i,j): j+1 <= i <= n}. So j=1: rows 2..3 => cells (2,1),(3,1). j=2: rows 3..3 => cell (3,2). So two rectangles covering L. For R, columns j=2..3: T_j^R = {(i,j): 1 <= i <= j-1}. So j=2: rows 1..1 => cell (1,2). j=3: rows 1..2 => cells (1,3),(2,3). So two rectangles covering R. Total 4 rectangles = 2n-2 = 4. Is that minimal? Could we do fewer? Possibly we could combine some cells across L and R? No, any rectangle crossing diagonal would include a hole. So we need to cover L and R separately. Could we cover L with fewer than n-1 rectangles? For n=3, L has 3 cells. Could we cover them with a single rectangle? The rectangle covering (2,1),(3,1),(3,2) would be rectangle from (2,1) to (3,2) covering cells (2,1),(2,2),(3,1),(3,2). That includes (2,2) which is a hole, so not allowed. So we need at least 2 rectangles. So lower bound holds.\n\nThus answer seems correct.\n\nNow we need to check reasoning validity. The solution uses reduction to diagonal holes (permutations). That's valid: we can permute rows and columns without changing the number of tiles needed because we can apply same permutation to tiles. Indeed, any arrangement of holes can be transformed to diagonal by permuting rows and columns; the ti