                "content": ".\n\n### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\boxed{4048}.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n1. **Permutation model:** The requirement that each row and each column contain exactly one uncovered unit square forces the uncovered squares to form a permutation matrix: there is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) (with \\(n=2025\\)) such that the uncovered squares are \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).  \n2. **Left/Right regions:** Split the covered squares into the “left’’ region \\(L=\\{(i,j):j<\\sigma(i)\\}\\) and the “right’’ region \\(R=\\{(i,j):j>\\sigma(i)\\}\\). After sorting rows by \\(\\sigma(i)\\), each of \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) becomes a Young diagram whose row‑lengths are the distinct numbers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \n3. **Inner‑corner lemma:** For each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) define the inner corner \\(c_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\in L\\); similarly for \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) define \\(d_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\in R\\). There are exactly \\(n-1\\) corners of each type.  \n   *Lemma:* Any axis‑aligned rectangle that avoids all holes can contain **at most one** of the points \\(\\{c_i\\}\\cup\\{d_i\\}\\); in fact a rectangle containing a corner of \\(L\\) cannot contain any corner of \\(R\\).  \n   Proof: If a rectangle contains \\(c_i\\) then its column interval lies entirely left of \\(\\sigma(i)\\); consequently all holes of rows in the rectangle lie to the right, so the rectangle lies wholly in \\(L\\) and cannot meet \\(R\\). The symmetric argument holds for a corner of \\(R\\).  \n4. **Lower bound:** Since each of the \\(2(n-1)\\) inner corners must belong to some tile and each tile can cover at most one corner, any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n5. **Construction attaining the bound:** Choose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (holes on the main diagonal). For each \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal tile covering the cells \\(\\{(i,j):i<j\\le n\\}\\) (a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle) and a vertical tile covering \\(\\{(j,i):i<j\\le n\\}\\) (an \\((n-i)\\times1\\) rectangle). These \\(2(n-1)\\) tiles are disjoint, avoid the diagonal holes, and together cover every non‑diagonal cell.  \n6. **Conclusion:** The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying the statement must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nHence the set of uncovered squares is a set\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nAll other squares are covered by tiles; tiles are axis‑aligned rectangles whose interiors are pairwise disjoint.\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Decomposition into left and right regions  \n\nDefine\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad\nR=\\{(i,j):j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\nClearly \\(L\\cup R\\) is the set of all covered squares, and \\(L\\cap R=\\varnothing\\).\n\nSort the rows according to the values \\(\\sigma(i)\\) in increasing order.  \nIf the rows are relabelled so that \\(\\sigma(1)<\\sigma(2)<\\dots <\\sigma(n)\\), then the row‑lengths of \\(L\\) become\n\\[\n|\\{j:j<\\sigma(i)\\}|=\\sigma(i)-1,\n\\]\nwhich are precisely the distinct numbers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  \nThus \\(L\\) is a Young diagram (Ferrers shape) with distinct row lengths.  \nAnalogously, after sorting rows in decreasing order of \\(\\sigma(i)\\), the region \\(R\\) becomes a Young diagram whose row‑lengths are again the distinct numbers \\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Inner corners  \n\nFor each \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) define the **left inner corner**\n\\[\nc_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\in L .\n\\]\nThere are exactly \\(n-1\\) such points because precisely one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\).\n\nFor each \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) define the **right inner corner**\n\\[\nd_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\in R .\n\\]\nAgain there are exactly \\(n-1\\) of them.\n\nThese points are the rightmost cells of the non‑empty rows of \\(L\\) and the leftmost cells of the non‑empty rows of \\(R\\), respectively.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Lemma: a rectangle contains at most one inner corner  \n\nLet a tile be a rectangle \\(T=I\\times J\\) where \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of rows and columns.  \nAssume \\(T\\) contains no hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\n*Suppose \\(T\\) contains a left inner corner \\(c_i\\).*  \nThen \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) and \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\in J\\).  \nSince the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) is not in \\(T\\), we must have \\(\\sigma(i)\\notin J\\); consequently \\(J\\subseteq[1,\\sigma(i)-1]\\).  \nFor any other row \\(i'\\in I\\) we also have \\(\\sigma(i')\\notin J\\); because \\(J\\) already occupies the whole interval \\([1,\\sigma(i)-1]\\), this forces \\(\\sigma(i')\\ge\\sigma(i)\\).  \nHence for every row of \\(I\\) the corresponding hole lies **to the right** of the whole column interval \\(J\\).  \nTherefore all cells of \\(T\\) lie left of the holes of their rows, i.e. \\(T\\subseteq L\\).  \nConsequently \\(T\\) cannot contain any point of \\(R\\), in particular no right inner corner \\(d_j\\).\n\n*The symmetric argument* shows that if \\(T\\) contains a right inner corner \\(d_i\\), then \\(T\\subseteq R\\) and it cannot contain any left inner corner.\n\nThus a rectangle avoiding all holes can contain **at most one** inner corner, and it cannot contain a corner of both types.\n\n∎\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lower bound on the number of tiles  \n\nEvery covered square must belong to some tile, in particular each inner corner must belong to a tile.  \nThere are \\(n-1\\) left inner corners and \\(n-1\\) right inner corners, altogether \\(2(n-1)\\) distinct squares.  \nBy the Lemma, each tile can cover at most one of these squares.  \nHence any tiling uses at least\n\\[\n2(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n---\n\