e two sets are disjoint.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Inner corners  \n\nFor each row with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) define the **left inner corner**\n\\[\nc_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\in L .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\nExactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\); consequently there are precisely \\(n-1\\) left inner corners.\n\nFor each row with \\(\\sigma(i)<n\\) define the **right inner corner**\n\\[\nd_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\in R .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\nExactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\); thus there are also \\(n-1\\) right inner corners.\n\nHence\n\\[\n|\\{c_i\\}|=|\\{d_i\\}|=n-1,\\qquad\n|\\{c_i\\}\\cup\\{d_i\\}|=2(n-1).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Lemma: a hole‑free rectangle contains at most one inner corner  \n\n*Statement.*  \nLet \\(T=I\\times J\\) be an axis‑aligned rectangle (where \\(I\\) and \\(J\\) are intervals of row and column indices) that does **not** contain any hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\). Then \\(T\\) contains at most one point of \\(\\{c_i\\}\\cup\\{d_i\\}\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume first that \\(T\\) contains a left inner corner \\(c_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\).  \nBecause the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\) is absent from \\(T\\), we have \\(\\sigma(i)\\notin J\\) while \\(\\sigma(i)-1\\in J\\). Hence\n\\[\nJ\\subseteq\\{1,2,\\dots ,\\sigma(i)-1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\nFor any other row \\(i'\\in I\\) the hole \\((i',\\sigma(i'))\\) must also avoid \\(T\\); thus \\(\\sigma(i')\\notin J\\). By (7) this forces \\(\\sigma(i')>\\sigma(i)-1\\), i.e.\n\\[\n\\sigma(i')\\ge\\sigma(i)\\qquad\\text{for all }i'\\in I.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\nConsequently every hole of a row belonging to \\(I\\) lies strictly to the right of the whole column interval \\(J\\); therefore \\(T\\subseteq L\\).\n\nNow suppose, contrary to the claim, that \\(T\\) also contains a second left inner corner \\(c_j=(j,\\sigma(j)-1)\\) with \\(j\\neq i\\) and \\(j\\in I\\). Then \\(\\sigma(j)-1\\in J\\). Using (7) we obtain \\(\\sigma(j)-1\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\), i.e. \\(\\sigma(j)\\le\\sigma(i)\\). Together with (8) (applied to \\(i'=j\\)) we get \