s no uncovered square, i.e.\n\\[\n(I\\times J)\\cap\\{(i,p(i)):1\\le i\\le n\\}=\\varnothing .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Separation into left and right parts)\n\nFor any $I\\times J$ satisfying (1) either $J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,\\min_{i\\in I}p(i)-1\\}$ or  \n$J\\subseteq\\{\\max_{i\\in I}p(i)+1,\\dots ,n\\}$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet $p_{\\min}=\\min_{i\\in I}p(i)$ and $p_{\\max}=\\max_{i\\in I}p(i)$.  \nSince the $p(i)$ are distinct, the interval $J$ cannot intersect the set $\\{p(i):i\\in I\\}$.  \nHence either $d<p_{\\min}$ (so $J$ lies completely to the left of all holes in the rows of $I$) or $c>p_{\\max}$ (so $J$ lies completely to the right). ∎\n\nDefine\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\},\\qquad R=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\nBy Lemma 1 every admissible tile is contained entirely in $L$ or entirely in $R$.\nThus any tiling of $C$ splits into a tiling of $L$ and a tiling of $R$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (A lower bound for $L$)\n\nFor each row $i$ with $p(i)>1$ set  \n\\[\na_i=(i,p(i)-1).\n\\]\nThe point $a_i$ belongs to $L$ and there are exactly $n-1$ such points (the unique row with $p(i)=1$ contributes none).\n\n*Claim.* No rectangle contained in $L$ can contain two distinct points $a_i$ and $a_{i'}$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume $a_i$ and $a_{i'}$ lie in the same rectangle $I\\times J$, with $i<i'$.  \nTheir column coordinates are $c_i=p(i)-1$ and $c_{i'}=p(i')-1$, and $c_i\\neq c_{i'}$.  \nWithout loss $c_i<c_{i'}$, so $J$ contains the whole interval $[c_i,c_{i'}]$.  \nConsequently $J$ contains the column $p(i)=c_i+1$.  \nSince $i\\in I$, the cell $(i,p(i))$ is a hole, contradicting that $I\\times J\\subseteq L$. ∎\n\nHence each $a_i$ must belong to a distinct rectangle of any tiling of $L$, so at least $n-1$ rectangles are required to cover $L$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (A lower bound for $R$)\n\nFor each row $i$ with $p(i)<n$ set  \n\\[\nb_i=(i,p(i)+1).\n\\]\nAnalogously to Lemma 2, the points $b_i$ lie in $R$, there are $n-1$ of them, and no rectangle contained in $R$ can contain two distinct $b_i$.  \nTherefore any tiling of $R$ needs at least $n-1$ rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Consequence\n\nAny tiling of the whole board $C$ uses at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation $p(i)=i$ for all $i$.  \nThen the uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells $(i,i)$.  \nConsequently\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):i>j\\},\\qquad R=\\{(i,j):i<j\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Tiles covering $L$.*  \nFor each column $j$ with $1\\le j\\le n-1$ define\n\\[\nT^{L}_j=\\{(i,j):i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n$T^{L}_j$ is a rectangle of size $(n-j)\\times1$, lies entirely in $L$, and the family $\\{T^{L}_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}$ consists of $n-1$ pairwise disjoint rectangles whose union is $L$.\n\n*Tiles covering $R$.*  \nFor each column $j$ with $2\\le j\\le n$ define\n\\[\nT^{R}_j=\\{(i,j):i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}.\n\\]\n$T^{R}_j$ is a rectangle of size $(j-1)\\times1$, lies entirely in $R$, and the family $\\{T^{R}_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}$ consists of $n-1$ pairwise disjoint rectangles whose union is $R$.\n\nThe two families are disjoint from each other because $L$ and $R$ are disjoint.  \nThus the collection\n\\[\n\\mathcal T=\\{T^{L}_j:1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{T^{R}_j:2\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\ncontains exactly $2n-2$ tiles, covers every covered square of the board, and leaves precisely the diagonal squares uncovered.  \nEach row and each column contains exactly one uncovered square (the diagonal one), as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion for $n=2025$\n\nFrom (2) the number of tiles is at least $2\\cdot2025-2=4048$, and the construction above shows that $4048$ tiles are sufficient.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
