r each row $i\\;(1\\le i\\le n)$ let $p(i)$ be the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row.  \nSince each column also contains exactly one uncovered square, $p$ is a permutation of $\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}$.\n\nDenote the set of uncovered squares by  \n\\[\nU=\\{(i,p(i))\\mid 1\\le i\\le n\\}.\n\\]\nA rectangular tile is a set $I\\times J$ where $I=\\{a,a+1,\\dots ,b\\}$ and $J=\\{c,c+1,\\dots ,d\\}$ are intervals of rows and columns, respectively.  \nA tile may be used only if it contains no uncovered square, i.e.\n\\[\n(I\\times J)\\cap U=\\varnothing .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nDefine the two regions of covered squares\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<p(i)\\},\\qquad R=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\nThus $L\\cup R$ is precisely the set of all covered squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Separation)\n\nLet $I\\times J$ satisfy (1).  Set $p_{\\min}=\\min_{i\\in I}p(i)$ and $p_{\\max}=\\max_{i\\in I}p(i)$.  \nThen either $d<p_{\\min}$ (so $J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,p_{\\min}-1\\}$) or $c>p_{\\max}$ (so $J\\subseteq\\{p_{\\max}+1,\\dots ,n\\}$).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause $J$ is an interval of columns and (1) forbids $J$ from containing any $p(i)$ with $i\\in I$, the interval $J$ cannot intersect the set $\\{p(i):i\\in I\\}$.  Hence $J$ lies entirely to the left of the smallest element $p_{\\min}$ or entirely to the right of the largest element $p_{\\max}$. \u220e\n\nConsequently every admissible tile is contained wholly in $L$ (if $J\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,p_{\\min}-1\\}$) or wholly in $R$ (if $J\\subseteq\\{p_{\\max}+1,\\dots ,n\\}$).  Thus any tiling of $L\\cup R$ splits into a tiling of $L$ and a tiling of $R$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Lower bound for $L$)\n\nFor each row $i$ with $p(i)>1$ define the point\n\\[\na_i=(i,p(i)-1).\n\\]\nThere are exactly $n-1$ such points (the unique row with $p(i)=1$ contributes none).\n\n*Claim.* No rectangle contained in $L$ can contain two distinct points $a_i$ and $a_{i'}$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume $a_i$ and $a_{i'}$ lie in the same rectangle $I\\times J\\subseteq L$, with $i\\neq i'$.  Write $c_i=p(i)-1$ and $c_{i'}=p(i')-1$.  Without loss of generality $c_i<c_{i'}$ (otherwise swap the roles).  Since $J$ contains both $c_i$ and $c_{i'}$, it contains the whole interval $[c_i,c_{i'}]$, and therefore the column $p(i)=c_i+1$.  Because $i\\in I$, the cell $(i,p(i))\\in I\\times J$, contradicting $I\\times J\\subseteq L$ (the cell $(i,p(i))$ is uncovered).  The case $c_i>c_{i'}$ yields a symmetric contradiction using $p(i')$. \u220e\n\nEach $a_i$ belongs to $L$, hence must be covered by some tile.  By the claim, each tile of $L$ can contain at most one $a_i$, so at least $n-1$ tiles are required to tile $L$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Lower bound for $R$)\n\nFor each row $i$ with $p(i)<n$ define the point\n\\[\nb_i=(i,p(i)+1).\n\\]\nAgain there are $n-1$ such points (the unique row with $p(i)=n$ contributes none).\n\n*Claim.* No rectangle contained in $R$ can contain two distinct points $b_i$ and $b_{i'}$.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe argument is identical to Lemma\u202f2, replacing $p(i)-1$ by $p(i)+1$ and $L$ by $R$. \u220e\n\nThus any tiling of $R$ needs at least $n-1$ tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Consequence\n\nAny tiling of the whole board $L\\cup R$ uses at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation $p(i)=i$ for all $i$.  Then the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells $(i,i)$.  Consequently\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):i>j\\},\\qquad R=\\{(i,j):i<j\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Tiles covering $L$.*  \nFor each column $j$ with $1\\le j\\le n-1$ define\n\\[\nT^{L}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=j+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n$T^{L}_j$ is a rectangle of size $(n-j)\\times1$, lies entirely in $L$, and the family $\\{T^{L}_j\\}_{j=1}^{n-1}$ consists of $n-1$ pairwise disjoint rectangles whose union is $L$.\n\n*Tiles covering $R$.*  \nFor each column $j$ with $2\\le j\\le n$ define\n\\[\nT^{R}_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,j-1\\}.\n\\]\n$T^{R}_j$ is a rectangle of size $(j-1)\\times1$, lies entirely in $R$, and the family $\\{T^{R}_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}$ consists of $n-1$ pairwise disjoint rectangles whose union is $R$.\n\nThe two families are disjoint because $L$ and $R$ are disjoint.  Hence the collection\n\\[\n\\mathcal T=\\{T^{L}_j:1\\le j\\le n-1\\}\\cup\\{T^{R}_j:2\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\ncontains exactly $2n-2$ tiles, covers every covered square exactly once, and leaves precisely the diagonal squares uncovered.  Each row $i$ contains the uncovered cell $(i,i)$ and all other cells of that row are covered by the vertical strips in column $i-1$ (if $i>1$) and column $i+1$ (if $i<n$).  Similarly each column $j$ contains the uncovered cell $(j,j)$ and all other cells are covered.  Thus the required condition \u201ceach row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square\u2019\u2019 is satisfied.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Conclusion for $n=2025$\n\nFrom (2) any tiling needs at least $2\\cdot2025-2=4048$ tiles.  The construction above shows that $4048$ tiles suffice.  Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
