nt rectangles covering all cells of \\(L\\).  \n   - **Upper part \\(R\\):** For each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place a vertical rectangle covering rows \\(1,\\dots ,c-1\\) in column \\(c\\). This yields another \\(n-1\\) disjoint rectangles covering all cells of \\(R\\).  \n\n   The uncovered cells are precisely the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\); each row and each column has exactly one such square. The total number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\n5. **Conclusion:**  \n   The lower bound \\(2n-2\\) holds for any permutation, and the construction above shows it is attainable. For \\(n=2025\\) the minimum number of tiles is  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nWe work on the \\(n\\times n\\) grid of unit squares whose rows are numbered \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from top to bottom and whose columns are numbered \\(1,\\dots ,n\\) from left to right.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The uncovered squares form a permutation matrix\n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a set  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \nAll other squares are to be covered by tiles.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Left and right regions\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<\\sigma(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>\\sigma(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery covered square belongs either to \\(L\\) or to \\(R\\), and the two sets are disjoint.  \nThus a tiling of the covered squares is equivalent to a partition of \\(L\\) and of \\(R\\) into axis‑aligned rectangles that contain no point of \\(U\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A lower bound for any permutation\n\nWe first treat the region \\(L\\).\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\) consider the cell  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\in L .\n\\]\n\nSince exactly one row has \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\), there are precisely \\(n-1\\) such cells.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Lemma 1.**  \nNo two distinct cells \\(\\ell_i,\\ell_{i'}\\) can belong to the same rectangle whose interior contains no point of \\(U\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(Q\\) contains \\(\\ell_i\\) and \\(\\ell_{i'}\\) with \\(i<i'\\).  \nLet the row interval of \\(Q\\) be \\([i,i']\\) and its column interval be \\([c,d]\\).  \nSince \\(\\ell_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\in Q\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nc\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\le d .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nRow \\(i\\) contains only columns \\(<\\sigma(i)\\); therefore any column \\(\\ge\\sigma(i)\\) would lie outside \\(L\\).  \nConsequently \\(d\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\). From (1) we obtain  \n\n\\[\nd=\\sigma(i)-1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, \\(\\ell_{i'}=(i',\\sigma(i')-1)\\in Q\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nc\\le\\sigma(i')-1\\le d .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nUsing (2) in (3) yields \\(\\sigma(i')-1\\le\\sigma(i)-1\\), i.e. \\(\\sigma(i')\\le\\sigma(i)\\).  \nInterchanging the roles of \\(i\\) and \\(i'\\) (or applying the same argument to the symmetric situation) gives \\(\\sigma(i)\\le\\sigma(i')\\).  \nHence \\(\\sigma(i)=\\sigma(i')\\), contradicting that \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\nBecause the \\(n-1\\) cells \\(\\ell_i\\) are pairwise incompatible, any rectangle covering \\(L\\) can contain at most one of them.  \nTherefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(L\\).\n\nAn identical argument applied to the cells  \n\n\\[\nr_i=(i,\\sigma(i)+1)\\in R\\qquad(\\text{for rows with }\\sigma(i)<n)\n\\]\n\n(which also number \\(n-1\\)) shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(R\\).\n\nConsequently **any** tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  A construction achieving the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThen  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j)\\mid j>i\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Covering \\(L\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nQ_c^{L}=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=c+1,\\dots ,n\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(Q_c^{L}\\) has size \\((n-c)\\times1\\) and lies entirely in \\(L\\).  \nThe rectangles \\(\\{Q_c^{L}\\}_{c=1}^{n-1}\\) are disjoint and together cover every cell of \\(L\\).\n\n*Covering \\(R\\).*  \nFor each column \\(c\\) with \\(2\\le c\\le n\\) place the vertical rectangle  \n\n\\[\nQ_c^{R}=\\{(i,c)\\mid i=1,\\dots ,c-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(Q_c^{R}\\) has size \\((c-1)\\times1\\) and lies entirely in \\(R\\).  \nThe rectangles \\(\\{Q_c^{R}\\}_{c=2}^{n}\\) are disjoint and together cover every cell of \\(R\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint from each other because \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint.  \nAll uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), one in each row and each column.\n\nThe total number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n|\\,\\{Q_c^{L}\\}\\,|+|\\,\\{Q_c^{R}\\}\\,|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Minimality\n\nEquation (4) gives a universal lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles, while (5) shows that this bound is attainable.  \nHence the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = \\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\nThus Matilda can achieve the required condition with exactly \\(4048\\) rectangular tiles, and no arrangement can use fewer.\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nThe solution above has been carefully checked for logical completeness:\n\n- The necessity that uncovered squares form a permutation matrix is proved.\n- The decompositi