mutation\n\\(\\sigma\\in S_{n}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{\\, (i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n \\,\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll other squares are covered by tiles; tiles are axis‑parallel rectangles whose interiors are\ndisjoint.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.   Left–right decomposition\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) write  \n\n\\[\na_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\qquad (0\\le a_i\\le n-1).\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nIn row \\(i\\) the uncovered cell is at column \\(\\sigma(i)=a_i+1\\); therefore the row is split\ninto\n\n* a **left part** consisting of the cells \\((i,1),\\dots ,(i,a_i)\\);\n* a **right part** consisting of the cells \\((i,a_i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\).\n\nThe left parts together form the left‑justified histogram  \n\n\\[\nH_{\\text{L}}=\\{\\, (i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le a_i \\,\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe right parts are the horizontal mirror image  \n\n\\[\nH_{\\text{R}}=\\{\\, (i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le n,\\;a_i+2\\le j\\le n \\,\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nEvery tile lies completely either in \\(H_{\\text{L}}\\) or in \\(H_{\\text{R}}\\); a tile cannot cross the\nvertical line \\(x=a_i+1\\) because that line contains a hole.  \nConsequently\n\n\\[\n\\#\\text{tiles}= \\#\\text{tiles covering }H_{\\text{L}}\n               +\\#\\text{tiles covering }H_{\\text{R}} .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThe two histograms are symmetric; the minimal number of tiles needed for each of them is the\nsame.  Denote by  \n\n\\[\nf(n)=\\min_{\\text{permutations }\\sigma}\\;\n      \\bigl(\\text{minimum number of rectangles partitioning }H_{\\text{L}}\\bigr).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThen the answer to the original problem is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,2f(n)\\,}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus we have to determine \\(f(n)\\) for \\(n=2025\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.   Upper bound – a construction with \\(\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) rectangles\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nL=\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nWe construct a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) for which the left histogram can be tiled with exactly\n\\(L\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  Recursive definition of the heights\n\nDefine a sequence \\((a_1,\\dots ,a_n)\\) recursively.\n\n*If \\(n=1\\)*, set \\(a_1=0\\).\n\n*If \\(n>1\\)*, let  \n\n\\[\nt=2^{\\,L-1}\\qquad (t\\le n-1<2t).\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nPlace the **largest** \\(t\\) numbers  \n\n\\[\nt,\\;t+1,\\;\\dots ,\\;n-1\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nin the **first** \\(t\\) positions (in any order).  \nThe remaining \\(n-t\\) positions receive the numbers  \n\n\\[\n0,1,\\dots ,t-1\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nagain in any order, but the order inside each block is obtained by applying the same\nprocedure recursively to the block.\n\nThus the set  \n\n\\[\nR_k:=\\{\\,i\\mid a_i\\ge 2^{k}\\,\\}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nis a **contiguous interval of rows** for every \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,L-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The tiling\n\nFor each \\(k=0,1,\\dots ,L-1\\) define the rectangle  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R_k=\n   R_k \\times\\bigl[\\,2^{k}+1,\\;2^{k+1}\\bigr] .\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nIts width is \\(2^{k}\\) and its height is \\(|R_k|\\).  \nBecause of (12), every row \\(i\\) belonging to \\(R_k\\) satisfies \\(a_i\\ge 2^{k}\\);\nhence the columns \\([2^{k}+1,2^{k+1}]\\) are indeed present in that row.\nConversely, if the \\(k\\)-th binary digit of \\(a_i\\) equals \\(1\\), then \\(i\\in R_k\\) and the\nrectangle \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) supplies exactly those columns.\n\nWriting each height \\(a_i\\) in binary,\n\\[\na_i=\\sum_{k=0}^{L-1} 2^{k}\\,\\varepsilon_{i,k},\n\\qquad \\varepsilon_{i,k}\\in\\{0,1\\},\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\nthe rectangles \\(\\mathcal R_k\\) cover precisely the columns corresponding to the\nbits \\(\\varepsilon_{i,k}=1\\).  The rectangles are pairwise disjoint (they occupy disjoint\ncolumn intervals) and together they cover the whole set \\(H_{\\text{L}}\\).\n\nHence the left histogram is tiled with exactly \\(L\\) rectangles, i.e.  \n\n\\[\nf(n)\\le L=\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil .\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.   Lower bound – at least \\(\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\\) rectangles are necessary\n\nAssume that a tiling of \\(H_{\\text{L}}\\) uses \\(m\\) rectangles.\nFor rectangle \\(j\\) let  \n\n* \\(I_j\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) be the set of rows it occupies (a contiguous interval);\n* \\(w_j\\) be its width (the number of columns it spans).\n\nEvery row \\(i\\) receives exactly the columns \\(\\{1,\\dots ,a_i\\}\\); therefore\n\n\\[\na_i=\\sum_{j:\\,i\\in I_j} w_j ,\\qquad i=1,\\dots ,n .\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\nWrite each width in binary,\n\\[\nw_j=\\sum_{k=0}^{L-1}2^{k}b_{j,k},\\qquad b_{j,k}\\in\\{0,1\\}.\n\\tag{17}\n\\]\n\nInsert (17) into (16) and reduce modulo 2.  \nFor the \\(k\\)-th binary digit \\(a_i^{(k)}\\) of \\(a_i\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\na_i^{(k)}\\equiv\\sum_{j:\\,i\\in I_j} b_{j,k}\\pmod 2 ,\n\\qquad k=0,\\dots ,L-1 .\n\\tag{18}\n\\]\n\nDefine the characteristic vector \\(v_j\\in\\{0,1\\}^{n}\\) of the interval \\(I_j\\) by  \n\n\\[\n(v_j)_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n1,& i\\in I_j,\\\\[2pt]\n0,& i\\notin I_j .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{19}\n\\]\n\nEquation (18) says that for each \\(k\\) the column vector\n\\(\\mathbf a^{(k)}=(a_1^{(k)},\\dots ,a_n^{(k)})^{\\!T}\\) lies in the linear span (over the field\n\\(\\mathbb F_2\\)) of the vectors \\(\\{v_1,\\dots ,v_m\\}\\).\n\nThe \\(L\\) vectors \\(\\mathbf a^{(0)},\\dots ,\\mathbf a^{(L-1)}\\) are linearly independent over\n\\(\\mathbb F_2\\): they are precisely the binary representations of the distinct numbers\n\\(0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\).  Consequently the subspace spanned by \\(\\{v_1,\\dots ,v_m\\}\\) has\ndimension at least \\(L\\).  Since at most \\(m\\) vectors can be linearly independent, we must have  \n\n\\[\nm\\ge L=\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil .\n\\tag{20}\n\\]\n\nThus any tiling of the left histogram needs at least \\(\\lceil\\log_2 n\\rceil\