families are disjoint and together cover all \\(n^2-n\\) squares not on the diagonal.  \n   - Total rectangles used: \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\).\n\n4. **Conclusion:** The lower bound and the explicit construction match, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\). For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###**  \n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nMatilda must leave exactly one uncovered unit square in each row and each column.  \nConsequently the set of uncovered squares is a **permutation matrix**: there exists a permutation  \n\\[\n\\sigma:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\nFor a fixed permutation \\(\\sigma\\) denote  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\\sigma(i)-1\\qquad\\text{(number of squares left of the hole in row }i\\text{)},\n\\]\n\\[\nR_i=n-\\sigma(i)\\qquad\\text{(number of squares right of the hole in row }i\\text{)} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A lower bound\n\nConsider an arbitrary tiling by axis‑parallel rectangles whose sides lie on grid lines and which does **not** cover any hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\).\n\n*If \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq1\\)*, row \\(i\\) contains at least one square left of the hole.  \nAny rectangle that contains a left‑hand square of row \\(i\\) must have its column interval entirely **to the left** of column \\(\\sigma(i)\\); otherwise the rectangle would also contain the hole \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), which is forbidden.\n\n*If \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq n\\)*, row \\(i\\) contains at least one square right of the hole.  \nAny rectangle that contains a right‑hand square of row \\(i\\) must have its column interval entirely **to the right** of column \\(\\sigma(i)\\).\n\nThus a single rectangle cannot simultaneously cover a left‑hand square and a right‑hand square of the same row.  \nConsequently\n\n* every row with \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq1\\) **and** \\(\\sigma(i)\\neq n\\) needs **at least two distinct rectangles** (one for its left side, one for its right side);\n* the unique row with \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) needs only a rectangle for its right side;\n* the unique row with \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\) needs only a rectangle for its left side.\n\nSince the permutation uses each column exactly once, there is exactly one row with \\(\\sigma(i)=1\\) and exactly one row with \\(\\sigma(i)=n\\).  \nHence any tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n2\\bigl(n-2\\bigr)+1+1 = 2n-2\n\\]\n\nrectangles. This is a **pure counting argument** establishing the lower bound.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  A construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Left of the diagonal.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{2,3,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i^{\\text{L}}=\\{(r,c)\\mid i\\le r\\le n,\\;1\\le c\\le i-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll rows in the interval \\([i,n]\\) have their holes at columns \\(i,i+1,\\dots ,n\\); the column interval \\([1,i-1]\\) lies strictly left of every such hole, so \\(R_i^{\\text{L}}\\) contains no uncovered square.  \nThe \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_i^{\\text{L}}\\}\\) are pairwise disjoint and together cover **every** cell \\((r,c)\\) with \\(c<r\\) (the whole left region).\n\n*Right of the diagonal.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) define the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nR_i^{\\text{R}}=\\{(r,c)\\mid 1\\le r\\le i,\\;i+1\\le c\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll rows in \\([1,i]\\) have their holes at columns \\(1,2,\\dots ,i\\); the column interval \\([i+1,n]\\) lies strictly right of each of those holes, so \\(R_i^{\\text{R}}\\) also avoids all uncovered squares.  \nThe \\(n-1\\) rectangles \\(\\{R_i^{\\text{R}}\\}\\) are disjoint and together cover **every** cell \\((r,c)\\) with \\(c>r\\) (the whole right region).\n\nThe two families are disjoint, and together they cover all \\(n^2-n\\) squares that are not on the diagonal.  \nThe total number of rectangles used is  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Optimality\n\nThe lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) proved in §1 holds for **any** permutation \\(\\sigma\\).  \nThe construction of §2 shows that the bound is **tight**: there exists a placement of the uncovered squares (the identity permutation) that can be tiled with exactly \\(2n-2\\) rectangles.  \n\nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Numerical answer\n\nFor the given grid \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4050-2=4048.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Hence the required minimum number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).**"
