n sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1. This is possible if sigma values are monotonic decreasing across rows i..j (i.e., sigma(i) > sigma(i+1) > ... > sigma(j)). In that case, sigma(k) will be between sigma(i) and sigma(j) (since decreasing). Actually if sigma is decreasing, then sigma(k) for k between i and j will be between sigma(i) and sigma(j). Since sigma(i) > sigma(j), sigma(k) will be in the interval (sigma(j), sigma(i)). That interval includes sigma(j)+1 up to sigma(i)-1, which is exactly the forbidden interval. So decreasing monotonic will cause sigma(k) to be in forbidden interval, making rectangle invalid. So we need sigma values for intermediate rows to avoid that interval, meaning they must be either all less than sigma(j) (i.e., even smaller) or all greater than sigma(i) (i.e., even larger). But sigma(i) is already the largest among those rows? Actually if sigma(i) is the maximum among rows i..j, then sigma(k) <= sigma(i) for all k, but we need sigma(k) not in (sigma(j), sigma(i)). So they must be <= sigma(j) (i.e., all equal or less than sigma(j)). But sigma(i) > sigma(j), so sigma(i) is not <= sigma(j). So sigma(i) violates condition? Wait condition is for each row k in interval, sigma(k) must be outside column interval. For k = i, sigma(i) is > sigma(i)-1? Actually sigma(i) is column sigma(i). The column interval is [sigma(j)+1, sigma(i)-1]. Since sigma(i) > sigma(i)-1, sigma(i) is outside interval (to the right). So sigma(i) is okay. For k = j, sigma(j) is < sigma(j)+1, so sigma(j) is outside interval (to the left). So both endpoints are okay. For intermediate rows k, sigma(k) must be either <= sigma(j) (left) or >= sigma(i) (right). So they cannot be between sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1. So sigma values for intermediate rows must be either all <= sigma(j) or all >= sigma(i). But sigma(i) is the maximum among them? Actually sigma(i) is the largest possible value among rows i..j if sigma(i) > sigma(j). But sigma(k) cannot be between sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1, so