4,4) (since (3,4) is hole). Not contiguous because row3 missing. Could cover (1,4)-(2,4) vertical 2x1 rectangle, and (4,4) alone.\n\nLet's count: (1,1) alone (1 tile), (3,1)-(4,1) vertical (1 tile), column2 3x1 (1 tile), column3 3x1 (1 tile), column4 vertical 2x1 (1 tile), (4,4) alone (1 tile). That's 6 tiles again.\n\nThus still 6.\n\nMaybe we can achieve 5 tiles by using a 3x2 rectangle (size 6) covering many squares. Let's see if any 3x2 region free of holes exists. For example rows1-3, cols3-4: squares (1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4),(3,3),(3,4). But (3,4) is hole. So not allowed.\n\nRows2-4, cols1-2: squares (2,1) hole, so not allowed.\n\nRows1-3, cols1-2: squares (1,1),(1,2? hole), (2,1? hole), (2,2), (3,1),(3,2). Contains holes at (1,2) and (2,1). Not allowed.\n\nRows2-4, cols3-4: squares (2,3),(2,4),(3,3),(3,4 hole),(4,3 hole),(4,4). Contains holes.\n\nThus no large rectangle.\n\nThus maybe minimal is 6 for n=4.\n\nBut we need to test n=5 maybe to see if lower bound holds. But we can suspect that the lower bound 2n-2 might be correct. However, we need to verify the lower bound argument more carefully. The solution's argument uses S_L and S_R sets of immediate left and right squares. They claim each rectangle can contain at most one element of S_L, and similarly for S_R. That's true. Then they claim \"Since the left-hand and right-hand regions are disjoint, the numbers of rectangles needed for them add up.\" This is the key step. The left-hand region is the set of all squares left of holes; the right-hand region is set of all squares right of holes. They are disjoint. But the number of rectangles needed to cover left region is at least n-1, and the number needed to cover right region is at least n-1. However, a rectangle that covers some squares in left region and some squares in right region would be counted in both counts? Actually the rectangle would be counted as covering some squares in left region, thus contributing to covering left region. But the lower bound for left region is based on covering S_L (immediate left squares). If a rectangle contains an element of S_L, it counts as covering that element. Similarly, if it contains an element of S_R, it counts for covering that element. So a rectangle that contains both an L and an R would count for both lower bounds, thus the sum of lower bounds might overcount that rectangle. However, the lower bound argument is that we need at least n-1 rectangles to cover S_L, and at least n-1 rectangles to cover S_R. If a rectangle covers both an L and an R, it would be counted in both sets, but we can't double-count the same rectangle for both lower bounds. The lower bound argument that total rectangles needed is at least (n-1)+(n-1) implicitly assumes that the sets of rectangles covering S_L and those covering S_R are disjoint. But they may overlap. So the lower bound is not rigorous.\n\nThus the reasoning may be flawed. However, does the lower bound still hold? Perhaps there is a stronger argument that any rectangle covering an L_i cannot also cover any R_j because of geometry constraints. Let's examine more thoroughly.\n\nGiven a rectangle that covers L_i = (i, sigma(i)-1). For it to also cover R_j = (j, sigma(j)+1), we need the rectangle's column interval to include both sigma(i)-1 and sigma(j)+1. For rectangle to avoid holes, for each row k in its row interval, sigma(k) must be outside that column interval. Let's consider the case where i < j. Then rectangle rows i..j. Suppose sigma(i) > sigma(j). Then column interval includes sigma(j)+1 up to sigma(i)-1. For any intermediate row k, sigma(k) must be <= sigma(j) or >= sigma(i). Since sigma(i) > sigma(j), sigma(k) cannot be between sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1. So sigma(k) must be either <= sigma(j) or >= sigma(i). But sigma(i) is the maximum among rows i..j? Not necessarily. But if sigma(i) is the maximum and sigma(j) is the minimum among those rows, then all intermediate sigma(k) must be either <= sigma(j) or >= sigma(i). Since sigma(i) is max, sigma(k) cannot be >= sigma(i) unless sigma(k) = sigma(i). So they must be <= sigma(j). So all intermediate sigma(k) must be <= sigma(j). That is possible if sigma(i) is large and sigma(j) is small, and all intermediate sigma(k) are even smaller (i.e., less than sigma(j)). This is possible if sigma values are decreasing sharply then increasing? Actually they must be less than sigma(j). Since sigma(j) is the smallest among those rows, intermediate rows must have sigma(k) <= sigma(j), which means they are also <= sigma(j). So they could be equal to sigma(j) (but sigma is a permutation, so cannot repeat). So they must be strictly less than sigma(j). So sigma(j) must be greater than all sigma(k) for k in (i,j). So sigma(j) is the second smallest among those rows? Actually sigma(j) is the smallest among rows i..j? Wait we assumed sigma(i) > sigma(j). So sigma(j) is smaller. For intermediate rows to have sigma(k) <= sigma(j), they must be <= sigma(j). Since sigma(j) is the smallest, they must be equal to sigma(j) or less, but cannot be equal because sigma is injective. So they must be less than sigma(j). But sigma(j) is the smallest among rows i..j (if we require sigma(k) <= sigma(j) for all k). So there cannot be any sigma(k) < sigma(j) because sigma(j) is smallest. So the only way is that there are no intermediate rows (i+1 = j). So rectangle covering L_i and R_j can only be possible if i and j are adjacent rows (i+1=j). Let's test that.\n\nIf i+1=j, then there are no intermediate rows. Then rectangle rows i..j includes just rows i and j. Column interval includes sigma(j)+1 to sigma(i)-1 (assuming sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1). For row i, sigma(i) > sigma(i)-1, so hole not in interval. For row j, sigma(j) < sigma(j)+1, so hole not in interval. So rectangle avoids holes. So rectangle covering L_i and R_j is possible when sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1 and i+1=j (adjacent rows). Similarly, if sigma(i) < sigma(j)-1 and i+1=j, rectangle covering R_i and L_j possible.\n\nThus a rectangle can contain both an L and an R only if the rows are adjacent and the sigma values are far apart (difference at least 2). In that case, the rectangle would be 2 rows tall and some columns wide.\n\nNow, does this allow us to reduce total number of rectangles below 2n-2? Possibly, but we need to see if we can pair up many L_i and R_j in such adjacent pairs to reduce count. For each such pair, we can cover two elements (one L and one R) with a single rectangle, reducing total count by 1 relative to covering them separately. Since there are n-1 L's and n-1 R's, if we can pair up all of them into (n-1) pairs, we could cover all L and R with (n-1) rectangles. But we need to respect adjacency constraints: each rectangle can cover at most one L and one R, and rows must be adjacent. So we need to pair up rows i and i+1 such that sigma(i) > sigma(i+1)+1 (or sigma(i) < sigma(i+1)-1). In other words, the permutation must have adjacent rows with sigma values that differ by at least 2 in the appropriate direction. Could we arrange a permutation such that each adjacent pair satisfies this condition, allowing us to pair all rows? Let's think.\n\nWe have n rows. There are n-1 adjacent pairs (i,i+1). Each rectangle can cover at most one L and one R, but each row i contributes either an L_i (if sigma(i)>1) or an R_i (if sigma(i)<n) (or both if sigma(i) is not at extremes). Actually each row i contributes both an L_i (if sigma(i)>1) and an R_i (if sigma(i)<n). So each row i (except possibly those with sigma(i)=1 or sigma(i)=n) contributes both an L and an R. So we have n-1 L's (since exactly one row has sigma(i)=1, no L for that row) and n-1 R's (since exactly one row has sigma(i)=n, no R for that row). So each row except the one with sigma(i)=1 contributes an L, and each row except the one with sigma(i)=n contributes an R. So there is a row that contributes only an R (the one with sigma(i)=1) and a row that contributes only an L (the one with sigma(i)=n). The rest of the rows