so a rectangle cannot be counted for both. But a rectangle can contain squares from both left and right region, but the lower bound counts rectangles needed to cover left region and right region separately, possibly counting the same rectangle twice. So the sum may overcount. However, perhaps they argue that any rectangle covering a left square cannot cover a right square because of the shape of the region. Let's examine: Suppose a rectangle contains a left square L_i = (i, sigma(i)-1). Since rectangle includes column sigma(i)-1, which is left of sigma(i). If rectangle also includes a right square R_j = (j, sigma(j)+1), then rectangle includes column sigma(j)+1, which is right of sigma(j). So rectangle's column interval includes both sigma(i)-1 and sigma(j)+1. If sigma(i)-1 < sigma(j)+1, then column interval includes sigma(i) and sigma(j) perhaps. But we need to avoid holes. Let's examine if it's possible for a rectangle to contain both a left square and a right square while avoiding holes. As we argued, it is possible if sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1 and rows i and j are adjacent and intermediate rows have holes outside interval. Let's try to construct a concrete example for n=5 to see if we can have a rectangle covering both a left and a right square.\n\nTake permutation sigma = (5,1,2,3,4) (i.e., sigma(1)=5, sigma(2)=1, sigma(3)=2, sigma(4)=3, sigma(5)=4). This is a cyclic shift. Let's compute L_i and R_i:\n\n- Row1: sigma(1)=5 >1 => L1 = (1,4). sigma(1)=5=n => no R1.\n- Row2: sigma(2)=1 => no L2, sigma(2)=1<5 => R2 = (2,2)\n- Row3: sigma(3)=2 => L3 = (3,1), R3 = (3,3)\n- Row4: sigma(4)=3 => L4 = (4,2), R4 = (4,4)\n- Row5: sigma(5)=4 => L5 = (5,3), R5 = (5,5?) Actually sigma(5)=4<5 => R5 = (5,5). Yes.\n\nNow can we find a rectangle covering L4 = (4,2) and R2 = (2,2)? That's same column 2, rows 2-4. Column interval is just column 2. Does this rectangle contain any holes? Holes in rows 2-4 are at (2,1), (3,2), (4,3). The rectangle includes column 2, rows 2-4, so includes (