                            "text": "We need to read the problem and solution, then apply hierarchical decision tree with Level 1 (answer correctness), Level 2 (reasoning validity), Level 3 (presentation issues). The solution claims answer: 4048 (2*2025-2). We need to verify if answer is correct. Let's analyze problem: \"Consider a 2025x2025 grid of unit squares. Matilda wishes to place on the grid some rectangular tiles, possibly of difference sizes, such that each side of every tile lies on a grid line and every unit square is covered by at most one tile. Determine the minimum number of tiles Matilda needs to place so that each row and each column of the grid has exactly one unit square that is not covered by any tile.\"\n\nInterpretation: We need to cover all squares except exactly one per row and per column, i.e., there are exactly n uncovered squares (n=2025), one per row and column. So uncovered squares form a permutation matrix (a set of n squares, each row and column exactly one uncovered). The rest of squares must be covered by non-overlapping axis-aligned rectangles (tiles) that align with grid lines. Tiles can be any size, but cannot overlap and cannot cover an uncovered square. We need to minimize number of tiles.\n\nSolution: They propose lower bound of 2n-2 = 4048 tiles, and provide construction achieving that using identity permutation (diagonal holes) and covering each row left of diagonal (one tile per row except first) and right of diagonal (one tile per row except last). That yields n-1 left tiles + n-1 right tiles = 2n-2 tiles. They argue lower bound: For each row, left of hole squares and right of hole squares each need at least n-1 rectangles. They define sets S_L and S_R of cells immediately left and right of holes (except first column and last column). They claim each rectangle can contain at most one element of S_L (or S_R). Since there are n-1 elements in each set, need at least n-1 rectangles for left region and n-1 for right region. Since