le includes both left and right squares relative to holes in different rows, then for some row the column interval may straddle the hole column, causing it to include the hole. Let's examine: Suppose rectangle columns are from c1 to c2. For row i, hole at sigma(i). If c2 < sigma(i) then rectangle is left of hole for row i. If c1 > sigma(i) then rectangle is right of hole for row i. If c1 <= sigma(i) <= c2 then rectangle includes hole. So for rectangle to avoid holes for all rows it covers, for each row i in the interval, either sigma(i) > c2 (hole to the right) or sigma(i) < c1 (hole to the left). That means sigma(i) must be either all > c2 or all < c1 for all rows in the rectangle. That would require the permutation to be monotonic across those rows. Since sigma is a permutation, it's possible to have a block where sigma(i) are all > c2 (i.e., all holes to the right of rectangle) or all < c1. But if rectangle includes rows where sigma(i) are on both sides of the rectangle columns, then it includes a hole. So a rectangle could be placed entirely left of all holes in its rows, or entirely right. So left region and right region are defined per row. But the left region includes all squares left of holes across all rows; the right region includes all squares right of holes across all rows. These two sets are disjoint. However, a rectangle could be placed entirely in left region for some rows and entirely in right region for other rows only if the rectangle's column interval is such that for rows in first part holes are to the right, and for rows in second part holes are to the left. But that would require the column interval to be both left of some holes and right of others, which is impossible because column interval is fixed. So rectangle cannot contain squares from both left and right region simultaneously because that would require crossing the hole column for at least one row. Let's formalize: Suppose rectangle columns are [c1, c2]. For any row i, if sigma(i) > c2, then rectangle is left of hole. If sigma(i) < c1, rectangle is right of hole. So rectangle can be left region for rows where sigma(i) > c2, and right region for rows where sigma(i) < c1. But if there exist rows i and j such that sigma(i) > c2 and sigma(j) < c1, then rectangle's column interval [c1,c2] must satisfy c1 <= c2. Since sigma(i) > c2 >= c1 > sigma(j), we have sigma(i) > sigma(j). That's possible. But does rectangle include any hole? For row i, hole sigma(i) > c2, so rectangle is left of hole. For row j, hole sigma(j) < c1, rectangle is right of hole. So rectangle does not contain holes for those rows. So rectangle could contain left region squares for some rows and right region squares for other rows, as long as the column interval lies between sigma(j) and sigma(i). But then rectangle includes columns c1..c2 that are between sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1. For row i, left region squares are columns < sigma(i). So rectangle columns are less than sigma(i) (since c2 < sigma(i)). For row j, rectangle columns are > sigma(j) (since c1 > sigma(j)). So rectangle can be left region for row i and right region for row j. However, does rectangle contain any uncovered squares? It doesn't contain sigma(i) or sigma(j). So it's okay. So left and right region are not globally separated; they are separated per row. But the solution's argument that left and right regions are disjoint is correct: they are disjoint sets of squares (the left region squares are those with column < sigma(i) for each row i; the right region squares are those with column > sigma(i) for each row i). Indeed these two sets are disjoint. So a rectangle cannot contain squares from both sets because that would require a square that is both left of its hole and right of its hole for the same row, which is impossible. But rectangle could contain left squares for some rows and right squares for other rows, but those squares belong to left region for those rows and right region for those rows. However, the rectangle's squares are still either left region squares for some rows, or right region squares for other rows. So the rectangle's squares belong to the union of left region and right region. The left region and right region are disjoint sets overall, but a rectangle can contain squares from both sets across different rows. The solution's argument that left and right regions are disjoint and thus the number of rectangles needed for them add up might be flawed: because a single rectangle could cover some left squares in some rows and some right squares in other rows, thus counting them separately may overcount. However, the solution's lower bound argument uses S_L and S_R sets of immediate left and right squares. They claim each rectangle can contain at most one element of S_L and at most one element of S_R. They also claim left and right regions are disjoint, so the rectangle counts for left and right add up. But they didn't claim that a rectangle cannot contain both an element of S_L and an element of S_R. Actually they treat them separately: they argue each rectangle can contain at most one element of S_L, and at least n-1 rectangles needed to cover S_L. Similarly for S_R. But a rectangle could contain one element of S_L and one element of S_R simultaneously, potentially reducing total number of rectangles needed. However, they claim left and right regions are disjoint, but that does not prevent a rectangle from covering one element from each region (since they are disjoint squares, a rectangle can contain both). But is it possible for a rectangle to contain both an L_i and an R_j? Let's examine: L_i = (i, sigma(i)-1) is left of hole in row i. R_j = (j, sigma(j)+1) is right of hole in row j. For a rectangle to contain both, its column interval must include sigma(i)-1 and sigma(j)+1. Suppose i != j. Then rectangle rows must include both i and j. The column interval must be from min(sigma(i)-1, sigma(j)+1) to max(sigma(i)-1, sigma(j)+1). If sigma(i)-1 < sigma(j)+1, then column interval includes sigma(i)-1, sigma(j)+1, and all columns between. Does it include any hole? For row i, hole is sigma(i). Since sigma(i) > sigma(i)-1, and sigma(i) may be <= sigma(j)+1? Actually sigma(i) could be less than sigma(j)+1 or greater. Let's consider possibilities.\n\nCase 1: sigma(i) < sigma(j)+1. Then column interval includes sigma(i) (since sigma(i) is between sigma(i)-1 and sigma(j)+1). Then rectangle includes hole (i, sigma(i)). Not allowed.\n\nCase 2: sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1. Then column interval includes sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1, but sigma(j) is less than sigma(j)+1, and sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1. Does rectangle include sigma(j) (hole for row j)? Since sigma(j) < sigma(j)+1, sigma(j) is left of column interval (c1 = sigma(j)+1?). Actually column interval is from sigma(j)+1 to sigma(i)-1 (if sigma(i)-1 > sigma(j)+1). Then sigma(j) is less than c1, so not included. So row j is fine. For row i, sigma(i) > sigma(i)-1, but sigma(i) might be > sigma(i)-1, but sigma(i) could be > sigma(i)-1 and also > sigma(i)-1 (obviously). But is sigma(i) inside the column interval? Column interval is [sigma(j)+1, sigma(i)-1]; sigma(i) > sigma(i)-1, so sigma(i) is greater than the upper bound, not included. So rectangle does not contain hole for row i. So rectangle could contain L_i and R_j if sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1 (i.e., sigma(i) >= sigma(j)+2). That is possible if sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1. So rectangle could contain left neighbor of hole in row i and right neighbor of hole in row j, provided the holes are far apart. So a rectangle could cover one left square and one right square from different rows, thus potentially reducing total number of rectangles needed.\n\nBut the solution's lower bound argument counts each rectangle covering at most one element of S_L and at most one element of S_R. But they didn't claim that a rectangle cannot cover both. However, they then sum the lower bounds: need at least n-1 rectangles for left region and at least n-1 for right region, so total at least 2n-2. But if a rectangle can cover both an L_i and an R_j, then that rectangle counts toward covering both sets, potentially reducing total needed. However, the lower bound argument must consider that each rectangle can cover at most one element of S_L and at most one element of S_R, but it could cover one of each. So the sum lower bound is not necessarily valid: we need to consider the possibility of rectangles covering both an L and an R. If we have at least n-1 elements in each set, we need at least max(n-1, n-1) = n-1 rectangles? Actually we need to cover all elements of both sets. Each rectangle can cover at most one L and at most one R. So each rectangle can cover at most 2 elements total (one from each set). So the total number of rectangles needed is at least ceil((|S_L|+|S_R|)/2) = ceil((2n-2)/2) = n-1. But the solution claims at least 2n-2. That seems too high. However, we must check if a rectangle can indeed cover both an L_i and an R_j while avoiding holes. Let's examine more carefully.\n\nWe need to find a rectangle that contains L_i = (i, sigma(i)-1) and R_j = (j, sigma(j)+1), and does not contain any hole (i.e., any (k, sigma(k)) for any row k in the rectangle). The rectangle's rows are from min(i,j) to max(i,j). Its columns are from min(sigma(i)-1, sigma(j)+1) to max(sigma(i)-1, sigma(j)+1). For each row k in that interval, we must ensure sigma(k) is not within the column interval. For rows other than i and j, we need to check. This may be impossible if the permutation sigma is arbitrary. But maybe we can choose sigma to make it possible. The lower bound argument is supposed to hold for any sigma (any arrangement of holes). So we need to check if for any permutation sigma, there exists a rectangle covering both an L and an R. If for some sigma, no rectangle can cover both an L and an R, then the lower bound may hold. But the lower bound must hold for the worst-case sigma? Actually the problem asks for the minimum number of tiles needed to achieve the condition (i.e., we can choose arrangement of holes and tile placements). So we are allowed to choose sigma to minimize number of tiles. So we can choose sigma that allows more efficient tiling. The lower bound must hold for any sigma (i.e., for any arrangement of holes). But we can choose sigma to achieve lower number of tiles. So we need to find the minimal possible number of tiles across all possible sigma and tilings. The solution's lower bound argument is supposed to be universal: for any sigma, any tiling must have at least 2n-2 tiles. If we can find a sigma and tiling with fewer tiles, then lower bound fails. Let's test if we can find a tiling with fewer than 2n-2. For n=2, trivial? Let's test small n to see pattern. For n=2, we have 2x2 grid. Need one uncovered per row and column, so uncovered squares must be a permutation of size 2: either (1,1) and (2,2) or (1,2) and (2,1). Let's examine minimal tiles.\n\nCase sigma = identity: holes at (1,1) and (2,2). The remaining squares are (1,2) and (2,1). These are not in same row or column, so they cannot be covered by a single rectangle (since rectangle covering both would be 2x2 covering holes). So each must be covered by separate tile. So we need 2 tiles. 2n-2 = 2*2-2 = 2. So matches.\n\nCase sigma = (1,2),(2,1). Holes at (1,2) and (2,1). Remaining squares are (1,1) and (2,2). They are also not in same row or column. So need 2 tiles. So minimal is 2. So lower bound holds.\n\nFor n=3: 3x3 grid. Need 3 holes. Let's test identity sigma: holes at (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). Remaining squares: left region squares: (1,2),(1,3) are right of hole? Actually left region squares are columns < sigma(i). For row 1, sigma(1)=1, left region empty. Row 2: left region squares (2,1). Row 3: left region squares (3,1),(3,2). Right region squares: row 1: (1,2),(1,3); row 2: (2,3); row3: none. So total left squares = 3 (n-1=2? Wait n-1=2, but left region squares count is 3? Let's compute S_L = immediate left squares: row2: (2,1), row3: (3,2). That's 2 elements (n-1). S_R = immediate right squares: row1: (1,2), row2: (2,3). That's also 2 elements. So S_L and S_R each size 2. The solution's lower bound says need at least 2+2=4 tiles. Let's see if we can tile with fewer than 4. Possibly we can tile with 3? Let's try to tile with 3 rectangles covering all squares except holes. Let's attempt to cover left region squares: (2,1),(3,1),(3,2). Right region squares: (1,2),(1,3),(2,3). There are 6 squares to cover. Could we cover them with 3 rectangles? Possibly each rectangle could be of size 2x1 or 1x2 etc. Let's think: maybe we can use rectangle covering (3,1),(3,2) (row3 left region). That's a 1x2 tile. Then rectangle covering (1,2),(1,3) (row1 right region) 1x2 tile. Then rectangle covering (2,1) and (2,3) cannot be done because they are not contiguous. So we need separate tile for (2,1) and (2,3). That would be 4 tiles total. Could we combine (2,1) with (3,1) in a vertical rectangle covering rows 2-3, column 1? That rectangle would contain (2,1) and (3,1). Does it contain any hole? Row2 hole at (2,2) column 2, rectangle column interval is column 1 only, so fine. Row3 hole at (3,3) column 3, rectangle column 1, fine. So vertical rectangle covering (2,1),(3,1) works. Then we still have (3,2) left region square, (1,2),(1,3) right region squares, (2,3) right region square. Could we cover (3,2) and (2,3) with a rectangle? That rectangle would be rows 2-3, columns 2-3. That includes (2,2) which is a hole! So not allowed. Could we cover (3,2) with a rectangle covering (3,2) alone? That's a 1x1 tile (allowed?). Tiles are rectangular, can be 1x1? Yes, unit squares are rectangles. So we could use a 1x1 tile for (3,2). Then we need to cover (1,2),(1,3) maybe with a 1x2 tile. And (2,3) with a 1x1 tile. That would be total: vertical rectangle (2,1)-(3,1) (1 tile), 1x2 tile (1,2)-(1,3) (1 tile), 1x1 tile (3,2) (1 tile), 1x1 tile (2,3) (1 tile) = 4 tiles. So still 4.\n\nCould we reduce to 3? Perhaps use a rectangle covering (1,2),(2,2),(3,2) vertical column 2 rows 1-3. But (2,2) is a hole. Not allowed. So cannot.\n\nThus for n=3 identity, minimal appears to be 4 = 2n-2 = 4. So matches.\n\nNow test other sigma: maybe a different permutation yields fewer tiles. Let's try sigma = (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Holes at (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). Let's list squares to cover. We'll try to find a tiling with less than 4 tiles. Let's see if we can tile with 3. Let's map grid:\n\nRows 1-3, columns 1-3.\n\nHoles:\n- Row1 col2\n- Row2 col3\n- Row3 col1\n\nRemaining squares:\nRow1: col1, col3\nRow2: col1, col2\nRow3: col2, col3\n\nWe need to cover 6 squares. Could we cover them with 3 rectangles each covering 2 squares? Possibly yes if we can pair them appropriately.\n\nPotential rectangles:\n- Row1 col1 and Row2 col1 are vertically adjacent (rows1-2, col1). Does rectangle include any hole? Row1 col1 is fine, row2 col1 is fine. Row1 hole at col2, not in column 1. Row2 hole at col3, not in column 1. So vertical rectangle covering (1,1)-(2,1) works.\n\n- Row2 col2 and Row3 col2 are vertical rectangle (rows2-3, col2). Row2 hole at col3, fine. Row3 hole at col1, fine. So rectangle covering (2,2)-(3,2) works.\n\n- Row1 col3 and Row3 col3 are not adjacent vertically because row2 col3 is a hole. So cannot cover both with a single rectangle. Could cover (1,3) alone as 1x1 tile.\n\nThus we have 3 rectangles: two vertical 2x1 rectangles and one 1x1 tile. That's 3 tiles total. That's less than 4. So minimal maybe 3 for this sigma. Let's verify no rectangle can cover (1,3) and (3,3) because (2,3) is a hole. Could we cover (1,3) with (1,1) maybe? Not contiguous.\n\nThus we have a tiling with 3 tiles. So the lower bound 2n-2 = 4 fails for n=3. But is this tiling valid? Let's double-check that each tile is a rectangle with sides on grid lines, covering squares without overlapping holes. Tiles: T1: covering squares (1,1) and (2,1) (vertical 2x1). T2: covering squares (2,2) and (3,2) (vertical 2x1). T3: covering square (1,3) (1x1). Are there any uncovered squares left? Let's list all squares: (1,1) covered, (1,2) hole, (1,3) covered. Row2: (2,1) covered, (2,2) covered, (2,3) hole. Row3: (3,1) hole, (3,2) covered, (3,3) uncovered? Wait (3,3) is not covered, not a hole. Did we miss (3,3)? Actually (3,3) is not a hole (hole at (3,1)). So (3,3) remains uncovered. But we need exactly one uncovered square per row and column. Let's check uncovered squares: holes are (1,2), (2,3), (3,1). Uncovered squares after tiling: (3,3) is uncovered, but row3 already has a hole at (3,1). So row3 would have two uncovered squares (hole and (3,3)). That's not allowed. The condition is that each row and each column has exactly one unit square not covered by any tile. The holes are the uncovered squares; we cannot have any other uncovered squares. So the tiling must cover all squares except the holes. So we must cover (3,3) as well. In our tiling, (3,3) is uncovered, violating condition. So we need to cover (3,3). Could we incorporate (3,3) into a tile? Perhaps we could extend T2 to include (3,3) as well? T2 currently covers (2,2)-(3,2). Could we extend to include (3,3) making a rectangle covering (2,2)-(3,3) (2 rows, 2 columns)? That rectangle includes (2,3) which is a hole. Not allowed. Could we add a tile covering (3,3) alone (1x1). That would be a fourth tile, making total 4. So minimal appears to be 4 again.\n\nThus the lower bound might hold for n=3. Let's verify if any tiling with 3 tiles covering all non-hole squares is possible. The total number of squares to cover is n^2 - n = n(n-1). For n=3, that's 6 squares. To cover 6 squares with 3 rectangles, each rectangle must cover exactly 2 squares (since 3*2=6). So each rectangle must be a domino covering two adjacent squares. The holes are at positions (1,2),(2,3),(3,1). We need to tile the remaining squares with 3 dominoes. This is a classic domino tiling problem. The board is 3x3 with three holes placed as a permutation. Is it possible to tile the remaining squares with dominoes? This is known to be possible only if the board has an even number of squares (which it does: 6) and the board is bipartite with equal numbers of black and white squares. The holes must be placed such that the remaining board has equal black and white squares. In a 3x3 board, there are 5 black and 4 white squares (or vice versa). The holes remove one black and one white? Let's compute parity. Color board like chessboard: (i+j) even = black. Count black squares in 3x3: positions where i+j even: (1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1),(3,3) = 5 black squares. White squares: (1,2),(2,1),(2,3),(3,2) = 4 white squares. Holes at (1,2) (white), (2,3) (white), (3,1) (black). So holes remove 2 white and 1 black, leaving 2 white and 4 black squares. That's unequal, so domino tiling impossible (needs equal numbers). So at least one tile must be larger than domino. So 4 tiles required. So lower bound holds.\n\nThus for n=3, 2n-2 = 4 is minimal.\n\nNow test n=4 perhaps to see if lower bound holds. For n=4, 2n-2 = 6. Could we tile with fewer? Possibly not. But we need to verify general argument.\n\nThe solution's lower bound argument is based on S_L and S_R sets each of size n-1, and each rectangle can contain at most one element from each set. But they didn't consider that a rectangle could contain one element from each set, thus covering two elements with one tile. However, they then claim left and right regions are disjoint, so numbers add up. But as we reasoned, a rectangle can contain both an L and an R from different rows, as long as it avoids holes. The question: is it possible for a rectangle to contain both an L_i and an R_j while avoiding holes? Let's examine more systematically.\n\nGiven permutation sigma, define L_i = (i, sigma(i)-1) for sigma(i) > 1. Define R_j = (j, sigma(j)+1) for sigma(j) < n. We want a rectangle covering both L_i and R_j. Let rows range from min(i,j) to max(i,j). Let columns range from min(sigma(i)-1, sigma(j)+1) to max(sigma(i)-1, sigma(j)+1). For rectangle to avoid holes, for each row k in that interval, sigma(k) must be outside the column interval. That is, sigma(k) < c1 or sigma(k) > c2 for all k. Since sigma is a permutation, the sigma values across rows may be interleaved. The rectangle's column interval is between sigma(i)-1 and sigma(j)+1. If sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1 (i.e., sigma(i) >= sigma(j)+2), then column interval is [sigma(j)+1, sigma(i)-1] (assuming sigma(j)+1 <= sigma(i)-1). For any row k in interval, sigma(k) must be either < sigma(j)+1 or > sigma(i)-1. In particular, sigma(k) cannot be between sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1 inclusive. So sigma values for rows in that interval must avoid that interval. This is possible if sigma values are \"outside\" that interval. For example, if sigma(i) is large, sigma(j) is small, and the sigma values for rows between i and j are either all less than sigma(j)+1 (i.e., small) or all greater than sigma(i)-1 (i.e., large). This is possible if sigma is monotonic decreasing across that range? Let's think.\n\nSuppose we have rows i<j (i smaller row index). For rectangle covering rows i..j, we need sigma(k) not in [c1, c2] for all k in [i,j]. If sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1, then c1 = sigma(j)+1, c2 = sigma(i)-1. So we need sigma(k) not in [sigma(j)+1, sigma(i)-1] for all k in [i,j]. That means sigma(k) must be either <= sigma(j) or >= sigma(i). Since sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1, the interval [sigma(j)+1, sigma(i)-1] is non-empty. So sigma(k) cannot be in that interval. So sigma values for rows i..j must be either all <= sigma(j) or all >= sigma(i). Since sigma(i) > sigma(j), sigma(i) is >= sigma(j)+2. So sigma(i) is >= sigma(j)+2. So sigma(i) is larger than sigma(j). So sigma(i) is in the \">= sigma(i)\" category (obviously). sigma(j) is <= sigma(j). So sigma(i) and sigma(j) satisfy the condition. For any intermediate row k, sigma(k) must be either <= sigma(j) (i.e., less than sigma(j)+1) or >= sigma(i) (greater than sigma(i)-1). So sigma(k) cannot be between sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1. This is possible if sigma values are monotonic decreasing across rows i..j (i.e., sigma(i) > sigma(i+1) > ... > sigma(j)). In that case, sigma(k) will be between sigma(i) and sigma(j) (since decreasing). Actually if sigma is decreasing, then sigma(k) for k between i and j will be between sigma(i) and sigma(j). Since sigma(i) > sigma(j), sigma(k) will be in the interval (sigma(j), sigma(i)). That interval includes sigma(j)+1 up to sigma(i)-1, which is exactly the forbidden interval. So decreasing monotonic will cause sigma(k) to be in forbidden interval, making rectangle invalid. So we need sigma values for intermediate rows to avoid that interval, meaning they must be either all less than sigma(j) (i.e., even smaller) or all greater than sigma(i) (i.e., even larger). But sigma(i) is already the largest among those rows? Actually if sigma(i) is the maximum among rows i..j, then sigma(k) <= sigma(i) for all k, but we need sigma(k) not in (sigma(j), sigma(i)). So they must be <= sigma(j) (i.e., all equal or less than sigma(j)). But sigma(i) > sigma(j), so sigma(i) is not <= sigma(j). So sigma(i) violates condition? Wait condition is for each row k in interval, sigma(k) must be outside column interval. For k = i, sigma(i) is > sigma(i)-1? Actually sigma(i) is column sigma(i). The column interval is [sigma(j)+1, sigma(i)-1]. Since sigma(i) > sigma(i)-1, sigma(i) is outside interval (to the right). So sigma(i) is okay. For k = j, sigma(j) is < sigma(j)+1, so sigma(j) is outside interval (to the left). So both endpoints are okay. For intermediate rows k, sigma(k) must be either <= sigma(j) (left) or >= sigma(i) (right). So they cannot be between sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1. So sigma values for intermediate rows must be either all <= sigma(j) or all >= sigma(i). But sigma(i) is the maximum among them? Actually sigma(i) is the largest possible value among rows i..j if sigma(i) > sigma(j). But sigma(k) cannot be between sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1, so 