an contain at most one element of S_L and at most one of S_R, but they don't consider that a rectangle could contain one of each. So the lower bound is too high. However, maybe they also argue that left-hand squares and right-hand squares are in disjoint regions, so a rectangle cannot contain both left and right squares simultaneously. But we argued that a rectangle can contain left squares in some rows and right squares in other rows. But perhaps they define left-hand region as all squares left of holes, and right-hand region as all squares right of holes. These are disjoint sets, but a rectangle covering squares from both sets would contain squares that are left of holes in some rows and right of holes in other rows. However, such a rectangle would cross the column of the hole for some row? Let's examine more concretely.\n\nSuppose we have rows i and j, with i < j. Suppose sigma(i) > sigma(j). Then the rectangle covering rows i..j and columns sigma(j)+1 .. sigma(i)-1 includes squares left of hole for row i? Actually for row i, left squares are columns < sigma(i). The rectangle's columns are from sigma(j)+1 to sigma(i)-1. Since sigma(j)+1 <= sigma(i)-1, these columns are less than sigma(i) (since sigma(i)-1 < sigma(i)). So they are left of hole for row i. So those squares are left region for row i. For row j, right squares are columns > sigma(j). The rectangle's columns are from sigma(j)+1 to sigma(i)-1. Since sigma(j)+1 > sigma(j), these columns are > sigma(j), so they are right region for row j. So rectangle includes left region squares for rows i..some? Actually for any row k between i and j, we need to check if rectangle's columns are left of sigma(k) or right of sigma(k). Since sigma(k) is between sigma(i) and sigma(j) possibly, the rectangle's columns might straddle sigma(k). If sigma(k) is between sigma(j)+1 and sigma(i)-1, then rectangle includes sigma(k), which is a hole. So to avoid holes, sigma(k) must be either <= sigma(j) or >= sigma(i). Since sigma(i)