oles inside. Since holes are at positions (i, sigma(i)). So we need to avoid holes in that rectangle. That means for each row i in [a,b], sigma(i) must not be in [c,d]. So sigma(i) must be either <c or >d for each i in that interval.\n\nIf we can find a block of rows 1..2 and columns 1..2 such that sigma(1) and sigma(2) are both >2 (i.e., >d). Then rows1-2, cols1-2 block is hole-free. For example, sigma(1)=3, sigma(2)=4. That gives holes at (1,3) and (2,4). Then block rows1-2, cols1-2 is free. Similarly, we need to ensure other holes do not lie in that block. That's satisfied because holes are only at those two positions. So we have a 2x2 block free.\n\nNow we need to tile the rest of the board (12 squares total). The block covers 4 squares, leaving 8 squares. We need to cover those with 4 more rectangles (to get total 5). Let's see if we can tile the remaining squares with 4 rectangles. The remaining squares are all squares except holes (1,3) and (2,4) and the block squares (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2). Actually block squares are part of the board, we need to cover them too. So we have 4 squares in block to be covered, plus other squares. So we need to tile all 12 squares (except holes). Let's try to tile as follows: Use a 2x2 block tile covering (1,1)-(2,2). That's one tile. Then we need to cover the rest of squares: rows1-4, columns3-4 except holes (1,3) and (2,4). Let's list all squares:\n\nGrid 4x4:\n\nRows/Cols:\n\nRow1: col1,2,3,4 -> squares: (1,1) (1,2) (1,3) hole? Actually (1,3) is hole, (1,4) free.\nRow2: (2,1) (2,2) (2,3) free, (2,4) hole.\nRow3: (3,1) (3,2) (3,3) free, (3,4) free.\nRow4: (4,1) (4,2) (4,3) free, (4,4) free.\n\nHoles: (1,3),(2,4). So free squares: all others.\n\nNow we have tile covering (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2) (2x2). Good.\n\nRemaining free squares: (1,4), (2,3), (3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(3,4),(4,1),(4,2),(4,3),(4,4). That's 10 squares. We need to cover them with 4 rectangles (since total tiles = 5). Average size 2.5. Could we cover with 4 rectangles