rows 3 and 4. Let's assign sigma(3)=1, sigma(4)=2. So permutation sigma = (3,4,1,2). Let's compute S_L: For each row i with sigma(i)>1, L_i = (i, sigma(i)-1). So:\n- Row1: sigma(1)=3>1 => L1 = (1,2)\n- Row2: sigma(2)=4>1 => L2 = (2,3)\n- Row3: sigma(3)=1 => no L3\n- Row4: sigma(4)=2>1 => L4 = (4,1)\nThus S_L = {(1,2),(2,3),(4,1)}. That's size 3 = n-1=3 (since n=4). S_R: For each row i with sigma(i)<n (i.e., <4), R_i = (i, sigma(i)+1). So:\n- Row1: sigma(1)=3<4 => R1 = (1,4)\n- Row2: sigma(2)=4 => no R2\n- Row3: sigma(3)=1<4 => R3 = (3,2)\n- Row4: sigma(4)=2<4 => R4 = (4,3)\nThus S_R = {(1,4),(3,2),(4,3)}. Also size 3.\n\nNow consider our tiling: block tile covering (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2) (2x2). This block includes L1 = (1,2) and L2? Actually L2 = (2,3) not in block. L4 = (4,1) not in block. So block contains L1. Also block contains R3? No. So block contains one element from S_L (L1) and maybe none from S_R. The big rectangle covering rows3-4 all columns includes squares (3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(3,4),(4,1),(4,2),(4,3),(4,4). This rectangle includes L4 = (4,1) (since L4 = (4,1)), also includes R3 = (3,2) and R4 = (4,3). So this rectangle contains L4, R3, R4. That's three elements from S_L∪S_R. So our tiling uses 2 rectangles that together cover all elements of S_L and S_R. Let's count: S_L = {(1,2),(2,3),(4,1)}. S_R = {(1,4),(3,2),(4,3)}. The block tile covers (1,2) (L1). The big rectangle covers (4,1) (L4), (3,2) (R3), (4,3) (R4). So we still need to cover (2,3) (L2) and (1,4) (R1). Those are covered by the singles (2,3) and (1,4). So total rectangles covering S_L∪S_R: block tile (covers L1), big rectangle (covers L4,R3,R4), singles (2,3) and (1,4) each covering one element. So total rectangles covering all S_L∪S_R = 4 rectangles. Lower bound says at least n-1=3 rectangles for left region and at least n-1=3 rectangles for right region. But we used 4 rectangles total, which is less than 6. So lower bound fails.\n\nThus the solution's lower bound is invalid. However, the solution's lower bound argument might be salvageable if they consider that each rectangle can contain at most one element of S_L and at most one element of S_R, but they then sum to 2n-2. But we have shown a counterexample for n=4 where total rectangles needed is 4 < 6. So the lower bound is false. Therefore the answer 4048 may not be minimal; perhaps a smaller number of tiles is possible for n=2025. Let's explore if we can generalize the construction we used for n=4 to larger odd n.\n\nOur construction used a permutation sigma where holes are placed in the first two rows at columns 3 and 4 (i.e., sigma(1)=3, sigma(2)=4) and the remaining rows have holes in columns 1 and 2 (sigma(3)=1, sigma(4)=2). This yields a large rectangle covering rows 3..n (or rows 3..4) all columns (since those rows have holes in columns 1 and 2, but the rectangle covering columns 3..n? Wait we used rows3-4, columns1-4 (full width). That rectangle includes holes? In rows3-4, holes are at columns 1 and 2. Actually in our permutation sigma(3)=1, sigma(4)=2. So holes are at (3,1) and (4,2). Our big rectangle covering rows3-4, columns1-4 includes those holes! Wait we need to check: The big rectangle we used covered rows3-4, all columns (1-4). That includes (3,1) and (4,2), which are holes! That would be invalid. Let's re-evaluate. Did we mistakenly think holes were at (3,1) and (4,2)? Actually we assigned sigma(3)=1, sigma(4)=2. So holes are at (3,1) and (4,2). The big rectangle covering rows3-4, columns1-4 includes those holes, which is not allowed. So our tiling is invalid! Let's double-check the earlier assignment. We originally set sigma(1)=3, sigma(2)=4, sigma(3)=1, sigma(4)=2. Indeed holes at (1,3),(2,4),(3,1),(4,2). So we cannot have a rectangle covering rows3-4 all columns because it would include holes (3,1) and (4,2). So our earlier tiling is invalid. Oops.\n\nThus the tiling we proposed is not allowed. Let's correct: The big rectangle must avoid holes. So we cannot cover rows3-4 all columns. We need to find a rectangle that avoids holes. Let's see if we can find a rectangle covering many squares while avoiding holes. For this sigma, maybe we can cover rows3-4, columns3-4 (i.e., bottom right 2x2 block). That block includes squares (3,3),(3,4),(4,3),(4,4). Are any holes there? Holes are at (3,1) and (4,2), so no holes. So we can have a 2x2 tile covering bottom right block. That covers 4 squares. Then we have block tile covering top left 2x2 (rows1-2, cols1-2) also hole-free? Holes at (1,3) and (2,4) are not in that block. So we can have a 2x2 tile covering (1,1),(1,2),(2,1),(2,2). That's another tile. So we have two 2x2 tiles covering 8 squares. Remaining squares: (1,4) (hole? Actually (1,4) is free), (2,3) free, (3,1) hole, (3,2) free, (4,1) free, (4,2) hole? Wait (4,2) is hole. Let's list all free squares after covering those two 2x2 blocks:\n\nAll squares:\nRow1: (1,1) covered, (1,2) covered, (1,3) hole, (1,4) free.\nRow2: (2,1) covered, (2,2) covered, (2,3) free, (2,4) hole.\nRow3: (3,1) hole, (3,2) free, (3,3) covered (by bottom right block), (3,4) covered.\nRow4: (4,1) free, (4,2) hole, (4,3) covered, (4,4) covered.\n\nRemaining free squares: (1,4), (2,3), (3,2), (4,1). That's 4 squares. Can we cover them with 2 tiles? Possibly two dominoes: (1,4)-(2,4) not allowed because (2,4) is hole. (1,4)-(1,3) hole. (1,4)-(2,4) not allowed. (1,4)-(2,4) includes hole. So can't pair (1,4) with any adjacent free square? Adjacent squares to (1,4) are (1,3) hole, (2,4) hole. So (1,4) is isolated; must be covered by a 1x1 tile. Similarly (4,1) adjacent squares: (3,1) hole, (4,2) hole. So (4,1) also isolated, must be 1x1 tile. (2,3) adjacent squares: (2,2) covered, (2,4) hole, (1,3) hole, (3,3) covered. So (2,3) also isolated. (3,2) adjacent squares: (3,1) hole, (3,3) covered, (2,2) covered, (4,2) hole. So (3,2) also isolated. So each of these four squares must be covered by a 1x1 tile. So total tiles: 2 (2x2 blocks) + 4 (singles) = 6 tiles. That's equal to 2n-2 = 6. So we didn't improve.\n\nThus our earlier claim of 4 tiles was invalid due to holes. So the lower bound may still hold for n=4.\n\nLet's try to find a permutation for n=4 that yields a tiling with fewer than 6 tiles. Perhaps we can use a different permutation that yields a larger rectangular region free of holes. For n=4, we need to avoid holes in a rectangle. Since there are 4 holes (one per row), we can try to place holes such that they are all in the same column? That's impossible because each column must have exactly one hole. So holes must be a permutation matrix. So we need to find a permutation sigma such that there exists a rectangle (rows a..b, columns c..d) that contains no holes. This is equivalent to finding a submatrix of the permutation matrix that is empty. For a permutation matrix, any submatrix that is a rectangle of size >1x1 may contain at most one 1 (hole) because each row and column has exactly one 1. But if we take a rectangle that includes multiple rows and multiple columns, it could contain multiple holes if the permutation places a hole in each row and column. However, we can avoid holes if the rectangle's rows and columns are chosen such that the permutation's 1's are outside. For a permutation matrix, there exists a rectangle of size a x b that is hole-free if and only if the set of rows and columns chosen do not intersect the positions of the holes. Since each row has a hole in some column, to avoid holes in a rectangle, we must ensure that for each row in the rectangle, its hole column lies outside the rectangle's column interval. Similarly, for each column in the rectangle, its hole row lies outside the rectangle's row interval. This is possible if we choose rows and columns such that the permutation maps those rows to columns outside the column interval, and the columns in the interval map to rows outside the row interval. This is essentially a \"bipartite subgraph\" problem. For a permutation, we can find a rectangle of size at least floor(n/2) x floor(n/2) that is hole-free? Not necessarily. For a random permutation, the largest empty rectangle might be small. But we can design sigma to maximize empty rectangle size.\n\nThe solution's lower bound uses S_L and S_R sets, which are of size n-1 each, and each rectangle can contain at most one element from each set. However, we found that a rectangle can contain both an L and an R from different rows, so the lower bound may not be tight. But perhaps the sum of lower bounds still holds because each rectangle can contain at most one L and at most one R, but we need to cover n-1 L's and n-1 R's, so we need at least max(n-1, n-1) = n-1 rectangles, but we also need to ensure that each rectangle can cover at most one L and at most one R, but we need to cover both sets. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover both sets is at least n-1 + something? Let's think: Suppose we have m rectangles. Each rectangle can cover at most one L and at most one R. So total L's covered ≤ m, total R's covered ≤ m. Since we need to cover n-1 L's and n-1 R's, we need m ≥ n-1. That's the only lower bound from that argument. So the lower bound 2n-2 is not derived from that constraint alone. The solution's argument that the left and right regions are disjoint and thus numbers add up is flawed. We need to examine if there is any other argument that forces each rectangle to be either left or right region exclusively, not both. They claim \"The left‑hand and right‑hand regions are disjoint, so the numbers of rectangles needed for them add up.\" But as we argued, a rectangle can contain squares from both left and right regions (as long as it avoids holes). However, does any rectangle that contains an L_i also contain any R_j? Let's examine more generally: Suppose rectangle contains L_i = (i, sigma(i)-1). Since rectangle includes column sigma(i)-1, which is left of hole in row i. For rectangle to also contain a right square R_j = (j, sigma(j)+1), rectangle must include column sigma(j)+1. So rectangle's column interval must include both sigma(i)-1 and sigma(j)+1. This implies sigma(i)-1 ≤ sigma(j)+1 (or reverse). So sigma(i) ≤ sigma(j)+2. If sigma(i) is much larger than sigma(j), then the interval includes many columns. The rectangle must avoid holes for rows i and j. For row i, hole at sigma(i) is to the right of sigma(i)-1, but if sigma(i) ≤ sigma(j)+1? Actually if sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1, then sigma(i) is inside column interval [sigma(i)-1, sigma(j)+1]? Let's sort: Suppose sigma(i)-1 ≤ sigma(j)+1 (i.e., sigma(i) ≤ sigma(j)+2). Then column interval is from sigma(i)-1 to sigma(j)+1. This interval might include sigma(i) if sigma(i) ≤ sigma(j)+1? Actually sigma(i) is one column to the right of sigma(i)-1. So sigma(i) is inside interval if sigma(i) ≤ sigma(j)+1. That is sigma(i) ≤ sigma(j)+1. Since sigma(i) and sigma(j) are integers, this condition is sigma(i) ≤ sigma(j)+1. If sigma(i) ≤ sigma(j)+1, then sigma(i) is ≤ sigma(j)+1, so sigma(i) is inside interval, thus rectangle would contain hole (i, sigma(i)). So to avoid hole, we need sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1. Similarly, for row j, hole at sigma(j) is left of sigma(j)+1, so if sigma(j) ≥ sigma(i)-1, then sigma(j) is inside interval. To avoid hole, we need sigma(j) < sigma(i)-1. So we need sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1 and sigma(j) < sigma(i)-1, which are equivalent. So condition is sigma(i) - sigma(j) ≥ 2. So sigma(i) ≥ sigma(j)+2. So the rectangle can contain L_i and R_j if sigma(i) ≥ sigma(j)+2 and rows i and j are adjacent? Actually we also need to consider rows between i and j. If there are intermediate rows, we need to ensure their holes are outside interval. That imposes additional constraints.\n\nThus a rectangle can contain both an L and an R if the sigma values differ by at least 2 and the rows are adjacent (or intermediate rows have holes outside interval). So it's possible.\n\nThus the lower bound 2n-2 is not guaranteed.\n\nThus the solution's reasoning is flawed. However, we need to check if the answer 4048 is indeed correct or not. The solution claims minimal number is 2n-2 = 4048. We need to verify if there exists a tiling with fewer tiles for n=2025. If we can find a construction with fewer tiles, then answer is wrong. If we cannot, answer might be correct but we need to verify the lower bound argument is valid. Let's analyze more deeply.\n\nThe problem is a known combinatorial geometry problem. The minimal number of rectangles needed to cover all but one square per row and column is known to be 2n-2. I recall a known result: In an n x n grid, covering all squares except a permutation matrix with axis-aligned rectangles (non-overlapping) requires at least 2n-2 rectangles. This is reminiscent of a problem about covering a board with rectangles while leaving a rook placement uncovered. I think the answer 2n-2 is indeed correct. The solution's lower bound argument may be correct but we need to verify the step about adding up left and right region counts. Perhaps there is a more subtle argument that ensures that any rectangle covering an L_i cannot also cover any R_j because of adjacency constraints across rows. Let's examine more thoroughly.\n\nDefine left region L = set of squares left of holes: for each row i, columns 1 to sigma(i)-1. Define right region R = set of squares right of holes: columns sigma(i)+1 to n. These two regions are disjoint. The solution's argument: each rectangle can contain at most one element of S_L (immediate left squares) and at most one element of S_R (immediate right squares). So to cover all left squares, we need at least n-1 rectangles; to cover all right squares, at least n-1 rectangles. Since left and right regions are disjoint, the rectangles covering left region are distinct from those covering right region, so total at least 2n-2. However, a rectangle covering left region could also cover some right region squares, but then it would be counted in both sets? But they claim left and right regions are disjoint, so a rectangle cannot be counted for both. But a rectangle can contain squares from both left and right region, but the lower bound counts rectangles needed to cover left region and right region separately, possibly counting the same rectangle twice. So the sum may overcount. However, perhaps they argue that any rectangle covering a left square cannot cover a right square because of the shape of the region. Let's examine: Suppose a rectangle contains a left square L_i = (i, sigma(i)-1). Since rectangle includes column sigma(i)-1, which is left of sigma(i). If rectangle also includes a right square R_j = (j, sigma(j)+1), then rectangle includes column sigma(j)+1, which is right of sigma(j). So rectangle's column interval includes both sigma(i)-1 and sigma(j)+1. If sigma(i)-1 < sigma(j)+1, then column interval includes sigma(i) and sigma(j) perhaps. But we need to avoid holes. Let's examine if it's possible for a rectangle to contain both a left square and a right square while avoiding holes. As we argued, it is possible if sigma(i) > sigma(j)+1 and rows i and j are adjacent and intermediate rows have holes outside interval. Let's try to construct a concrete example for n=5 to see if we can have a rectangle covering both a left and a right square.\n\nTake permutation sigma = (5,1,2,3,4) (i.e., sigma(1)=5, sigma(2)=1, sigma(3)=2, sigma(4)=3, sigma(5)=4). This is a cyclic shift. Let's compute L_i and R_i:\n\n- Row1: sigma(1)=5 >1 => L1 = (1,4). sigma(1)=5=n => no R1.\n- Row2: sigma(2)=1 => no L2, sigma(2)=1<5 => R2 = (2,2)\n- Row3: sigma(3)=2 => L3 = (3,1), R3 = (3,3)\n- Row4: sigma(4)=3 => L4 = (4,2), R4 = (4,4)\n- Row5: sigma(5)=4 => L5 = (5,3), R5 = (5,5?) Actually sigma(5)=4<5 => R5 = (5,5). Yes.\n\nNow can we find a rectangle covering L4 = (4,2) and R2 = (2,2)? That's same column 2, rows 2-4. Column interval is just column 2. Does this rectangle contain any holes? Holes in rows 2-4 are at (2,1), (3,2), (4,3). The rectangle includes column 2, rows 2-4, so includes (2,2) (R2), (3,2) (hole! because sigma(3)=2). So rectangle includes a hole, not allowed. So that rectangle fails.\n\nTry rectangle covering L3 = (3,1) and R5 = (5,5). These are far apart, not possible.\n\nTry rectangle covering L1 = (1,4) and R3 = (3,3). L1 column 4, R3 column 3. Column interval 3-4. Rows 1-3. Holes: (1,5) not in interval, (2,1) not in interval, (3,2) not in interval. So rectangle rows1-3, cols3-4 includes squares (1,3),(1,4),(2,3),(2,4),(3,3),(3,4). Does it include any hole? Holes are at (1,5) (outside), (2,1) (outside), (3,2) (outside), (4,3) (row4 not included), (5,4) (row5 not included). So no holes! Wait we need to check holes: sigma(1)=5 => hole (1,5) not in rectangle. sigma(2)=1 => hole (2,1) not in rectangle. sigma(3)=2 => hole (3,2) not in rectangle. So rectangle containing rows 1-3, cols 3-4 is hole-free. It contains L1 = (1,4) (since sigma(1)-1 = 4) and R3 = (3,3) (since sigma(3)+1 = 3). So indeed rectangle covers both an L and an R. So a rectangle can contain both L and R. This rectangle also includes many other squares (including left region squares for row2? Actually row2 left region squares are columns < sigma(2)=1 => none, right region squares are columns >1 => columns 2-5, but rectangle includes columns 3-4, so includes some right squares for row2 (2,3),(2,4) which are not holes). So rectangle covers many squares.\n\nThus the lower bound argument that left and right regions are disjoint and thus counts add up is flawed. So the solution's lower bound is invalid.\n\nThus answer 4048 is not proven. But is it actually the minimal number? Let's try to see if we can find a tiling with fewer than 2n-2 for n=2025. Perhaps we can adapt the above example to large n to reduce tile count. The example for n=5 gave a rectangle covering rows1-3, cols3-4 (size 3x2 = 6 squares). This rectangle covers both left and right squares, reducing tile count. Could we design a permutation sigma that yields large rectangles covering many squares, thus reducing t