ed is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2 .\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound and the construction match, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n---\n\n**### Detailed Solution ###  \n\nLet the grid consist of the rows \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) and columns \\(\\{1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\) with \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles is a family of axis‑aligned rectangles whose interiors are pairwise disjoint and whose union consists of all unit squares except a set \\(U\\) of uncovered squares.  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Structure of the uncovered squares  \n\nThe requirement “each row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square’’ means that  \n\n\\[\n|U\\cap\\{i\\}\\times\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}|=1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad \n|U\\cap\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\times\\{j\\}|=1\n\\]\n\nfor every row \\(i\\) and every column \\(j\\).  \nHence \\(U\\) consists of exactly \\(n\\) squares, one in each row and one in each column; therefore there is a permutation \\(\\sigma\\) of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(i,\\sigma(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Two families of “adjacent’’ cells  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i,\\sigma(i)-1), & \\text{if }\\sigma(i)>1,\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{undefined}, & \\text{if }\\sigma(i)=1,\n\\end{cases}\n\\qquad\nR_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i,\\sigma(i)+1), & \\text{if }\\sigma(i)<n,\\\\[2pt]\n\\text{undefined}, & \\text{if }\\sigma(i)=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) is the rightmost square **left** of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\); \\(R_i\\) is the leftmost square **right** of it.  \nBecause exactly one uncovered square lies in column 1, there is precisely one index \\(i_0\\) with \\(\\sigma(i_0)=1\\); consequently the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{L_i\\mid \\sigma(i)>1\\}\n\\]\n\nhas cardinality \\(n-1\\).  \nSimilarly, because exactly one uncovered square lies in column \\(n\\), the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R=\\{R_i\\mid \\sigma(i)<n\\}\n\\]\n\nalso has cardinality \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  A tile can contain at most one cell of \\(\\mathcal L\\) (or \\(\\mathcal R\\))\n\nConsider any tile \\(T\\).  Since a tile is a rectangle, there exist integers  \n\n\\[\nr_1\\le r_2,\\qquad c_1\\le c_2\n\\]\n\nsuch that  \n\n\\[\nT=\\{(r,c)\\mid r_1\\le r\\le r_2,\\;c_1\\le c\\le c_2\\}.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(T\\) must avoid every uncovered square \\((i,\\sigma(i))\\), for each row \\(r\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) the whole column interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\) lies either entirely **to the left** of \\(\\sigma(r)\\) (i.e. \\(c_2<\\sigma(r)\\)) or entirely **to the right** of \\(\\sigma(r)\\) (i.e. \\(c_1>\\sigma(r)\\)).  \nIf for two rows the tile were on opposite sides of their respective uncovered squares, the column interval would intersect an uncovered square, which is impossible.  Hence the tile is uniformly on one side of all holes in its rows.\n\n*Suppose \\(T\\) lies to the left of the holes of its rows.*  \nThen for every \\(r\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) we have \\(c_2<\\sigma(r)\\).  \nIf \\(T\\) contains a left‑adjacent cell \\(L_i=(i,\\sigma(i)-1)\\) (so \\(\\sigma(i)>1\\)), then \\(c_2\\ge\\sigma(i)-1\\).  \nCombining the two inequalities gives  \n\n\\[\nc_2=\\sigma(i)-1\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\sigma(i)-1<\\sigma(r)\\ \\text{for all }r\\in[r_1,r_2].\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\sigma(i)\\) is the **minimum** of the values \\(\\sigma(r)\\) over the rows of the tile.  \nIf \\(T\\) contained a second left‑adjacent cell \\(L_k\\) with \\(k\\neq i\\), then (2) would force \\(\\sigma(k)=\\sigma(i)\\), contradicting the fact that \\(\\sigma\\) is a permutation.  \nHence a left‑side tile contains **at most one** element of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*The same argument applied to a tile that lies to the right of the holes* shows that such a tile can contain at most one element of \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\nFinally, a tile cannot contain simultaneously a cell of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and a cell of \\(\\mathcal R\\), because that would require the tile to be simultaneously left of the hole in one row and right of the hole in another row, which is impossible as argued above.\n\nConsequently\n\n* each element of \\(\\mathcal L\\) forces a distinct tile,  \n* each element of \\(\\mathcal R\\) forces a distinct tile,  \n\nand the two families of tiles are disjoint.  Therefore any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nThus the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\) for all \\(i\\).  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i>1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nand for each row \\(i\\) with \\(i<n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll these rectangles are pairwise disjoint, each is a legitimate tile (its sides lie on grid lines), and together they cover every square except the diagonal ones.  \nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left rectangles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right rectangles}}=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (3) is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion for the given size  \n\nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
