s **at most one** element of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*The same argument applied to a tile that lies to the right of the holes* shows that such a tile can contain at most one element of \\(\\mathcal R\\).\n\nFinally, a tile cannot contain simultaneously a cell of \\(\\mathcal L\\) and a cell of \\(\\mathcal R\\), because that would require the tile to be simultaneously left of the hole in one row and right of the hole in another row, which is impossible as argued above.\n\nConsequently\n\n* each element of \\(\\mathcal L\\) forces a distinct tile,  \n* each element of \\(\\mathcal R\\) forces a distinct tile,  \n\nand the two families of tiles are disjoint.  Therefore any admissible tiling uses at least  \n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal L|+|\\mathcal R|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nTake the permutation \\(\\sigma(i)=i\\) (the main diagonal).  \nThus the uncovered squares are \\((i,i)\\) for all \\(i\\).  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) with \\(i>1\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{left}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le i-1\\},\n\\]\n\nand for each row \\(i\\) with \\(i<n\\) place the horizontal rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT_i^{\\text{right}}=\\{(i,j)\\mid i+1\\le j\\le n\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll these rectangles are pairwise disjoint, each is a legitimate tile (its sides lie on grid lines), and together they cover every square except the diagonal ones.  \nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{left rectangles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{right rectangles}}=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (3) is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion for the given size  \n\nWith \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n\\text{minimum number of tiles}=2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\n\nHence the answer is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
