ally, the same argument as for Lemma\u202f1 shows that a rectangle can contain at most one cell of \\(R\\).  \n     Hence at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all cells of \\(R\\).\n\n   - **Disjointness of the two families.**  \n     Any rectangle that contains a cell of \\(L\\) lies entirely on the left side of the hole in its row, while a rectangle that contains a cell of \\(R\\) lies entirely on the right side of that hole.  \n     Therefore a single rectangle cannot contain a cell from both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\).  \n     Consequently the rectangles required for \\(L\\) and those required for \\(R\\) are distinct, giving a total lower bound  \n\n     \\[\n     (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n     \\]\n\n3. **Construction attaining \\(2n-2\\).**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n\n   - For each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place a **horizontal** tile covering  \n\n     \\[\n     H_i:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) rectangle that occupies all squares to the right of the diagonal hole in that row.\n\n   - For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place a **vertical** tile covering  \n\n     \\[\n     V_j:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n     \\]\n\n     i.e. an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle that occupies all squares below the diagonal hole in that column.\n\n   The horizontal tiles lie in distinct rows, the vertical tiles lie in distinct columns, and a horizontal tile \\(H_i\\) and a vertical tile \\(V_j\\) never intersect because their intersection would require simultaneously \\(j>i\\) (to belong to \\(H_i\\)) and \\(i>j\\) (to belong to \\(V_j\\)), which is impossible.  \n   Hence the tiles are pairwise disjoint.  \n\n   Every cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\) belongs either to some \\(H_i\\) (if \\(j>i\\)) or to some \\(V_j\\) (if \\(i>j\\)).  \n   The only uncovered cells are the diagonal ones \\((i,i)\\), exactly one per row and per column.  \n\n   The number of tiles used is  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\{H_i\\}|+|\\{V_j\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower\u2011bound argument shows that fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles are impossible, while the explicit construction uses exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \n   Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(2n-2\\).  \n   Substituting \\(n=2025\\) yields  \n\n   \\[\n   2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n   \\]\n\n   Hence the answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  We work in general for an \\(n\\times n\\) board and later substitute \\(n=2025\\).\n\n#### 1.  Form of the uncovered squares  \n\nBecause each row must contain exactly one uncovered unit square and each column must contain exactly one uncovered unit square, the set of uncovered squares is a *permutation matrix*: there exists a bijection  \n\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nsuch that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is the cell  \n\n\\[\nu_i=(i,p(i)).\n\\]\n\nNo two uncovered squares share a row or a column, and there are exactly \\(n\\) uncovered squares.\n\n#### 2.  A lower bound of \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nFor each row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:=p(i)-1,\\qquad r_i:=n-p(i).\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\ell_i\\) is the number of squares to the **left** of the hole in row \\(i\\), and \\(r_i\\) is the number of squares to the **right** of that hole.  \nSince \\(p\\) is a bijection, the multiset \\(\\{\\ell_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\); likewise \\(\\{r_i\\}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 2.1  Cells that force at least \\(n-1\\) tiles  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL:=\\{(i,\\ell_i)\\mid \\ell_i>0\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each row with \\(\\ell_i>0\\) the cell \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) is the **rightmost** square of the left part of that row.  \nBecause the numbers \\(\\ell_i\\) are distinct, the cells of \\(L\\) lie in distinct columns.\n\n**Lemma 1.** *A rectangle that avoids all uncovered squares can contain at most one cell of \\(L\\).*\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(Q\\) contains two distinct cells \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) and \\((i',\\ell_{i'})\\) with \\(\\ell_i<\\ell_{i'}\\).  \nThe column interval of \\(Q\\) therefore contains the column \\(\\ell_i+1=p(i)\\).  \nConsequently \\(Q\\) also contains the cell \\((i,p(i))\\), which is precisely the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).  \nSince a tile may not cover an uncovered square, this is impossible. \u220e\n\nBecause \\(|L|=n-1\\) (exactly one row has \\(\\ell_i=0\\)), Lemma\u202f1 implies that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover all cells of \\(L\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 2.2  Symmetric cells  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nR:=\\{(i,p(i)+1)\\mid r_i>0\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each row with \\(r_i>0\\) the cell \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) is the **leftmost** square of the right part of that row.  \nThe columns \\(p(i)+1\\) are distinct (they run through \\(\\{2,\\dots ,n\\}\\)), so the cells of \\(R\\) also lie in distinct columns.\n\nReflect the board horizontally (replace column \\(j\\) by column \\(n+1-j\\)).  \nUnder this reflection the set \\(R\\) becomes a set of the same type as \\(L\\); hence the same argument as in Lemma\u202f1 shows:\n\n**Lemma 2.** *A rectangle that avoids all uncovered squares can contain at most one cell of \\(R\\).*\n\nThus at least another \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover all cells of \\(R\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 2.3  Disjointness of the two families  \n\nIn any row \\(i\\) the cells \\((i,\\ell_i)\\) (if they exist) lie strictly to the left of the hole \\((i,p(i))\\), while the cells \\((i,p(i)+1)\\) (if they exist) lie strictly to the right of that hole.  \nTherefore a single rectangle cannot contain a cell from both \\(L\\) and \\(R\\); otherwise it would have to cross the hole in that row, which is forbidden.  \n\nConsequently the rectangles needed for \\(L\\) and those needed for \\(R\\) are distinct, giving a total lower bound\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n#### 3.  Construction with exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\) for \\(i=1,\\dots ,n\\).\n\n- **Horizontal tiles.**  \n  For each row \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  H_i:=\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  which is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile covering all squares to the right of the diagonal hole in that row.\n\n- **Vertical tiles.**  \n  For each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the rectangle  \n\n  \\[\n  V_j:=\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\le n\\},\n  \\]\n\n  which is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile covering all squares below the diagonal hole in that column.\n\n**Verification.**  \n\n1. *Disjointness.*  \n   The horizontal tiles occupy distinct rows, and the vertical tiles occupy distinct columns.  \n   If a cell \\((i,j)\\) belonged to both some \\(H_i\\) and some \\(V_j\\), we would need simultaneously \\(j>i\\) (to be in \\(H_i\\)) and \\(i>j\\) (to be in \\(V_j\\)), an impossibility. Hence the tiles are pairwise disjoint.\n\n2. *Coverage.*  \n   For any cell \\((i,j)\\) with \\(i\\neq j\\):\n   - If \\(j>i\\) then \\((i,j)\\in H_i\\).\n   - If \\(i>j\\) then \\((i,j)\\in V_j\\).\n   Thus every non\u2011diagonal cell is covered by exactly one tile.\n\n3. *Uncovered squares.*  \n   The only cells not belonging to any tile are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n   Consequently each row and each column contains exactly one uncovered unit square, as required.\n\nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n|\\{H_i\\}|+|\\{V_j\\}|=(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\]\n\n#### 4.  Minimality  \n\nThe lower\u2011bound argument of Section\u202f2 shows that any tiling satisfying the row\u2011 and column\u2011hole condition must use at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nThe construction of Section\u202f3 achieves exactly \\(2n-2\\) tiles.  \nTherefore the minimum possible number of tiles equals \\(2n-2\\).\n\nFor the original board with \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n